alculus#activation","content":"Activation","locale":"en"},"4536":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus-12","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus#deactivation","content":"Deactivation","locale":"en"},"4537":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus-13","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus#confinement-423","content":"Confinement (§4.2.3)","locale":"en"},"4538":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus-14","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus#table-1--rules-as-writes","content":"Table 1 – rules as writes","locale":"en"},"4539":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus-15","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus#engineering-take-aways-for-an-implementation","content":"Engineering take-aways for an implementation","locale":"en"},"4540":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus-16","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus","content":"This page summarises §4.1–4.2 of the paper (arXiv:2608.25512). It is the reference for the *state machine* the Rust core must implement. For how Cordis realises it, see Fiber lifecycle in the Cordis reference; for kardia's state machine, see Lifecycle.","locale":"en"},"4541":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus-17","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus#definition-48--component","content":"`d`: the coeffect **specification** (the keys required).","locale":"en"},"4542":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus-18","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus#definition-48--component","content":"`p`: the coeffect **provision** (`Set(K)`, the keys it *may* install; no key outside `p` may be installed by its effect function).","locale":"en"},"4543":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus-19","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus#definition-48--component","content":"`e`: a witnessed **effect iterator** (witnessed up to `≃_{d∪p}`): the effects contributed while active, plus their inverses.","locale":"en"},"4544":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus-20","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus#definition-49--fiber","content":"A fiber instantiating `(d, p, e)` is `⟨d, p, e, π, σ, τ, θ⟩`:","locale":"en"},"4545":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus-21","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus#definition-49--fiber","content":"Field","locale":"en"},"4546":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus-22","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus#definition-49--fiber","content":"Meaning","locale":"en"},"4547":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus-23","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus#definition-49--fiber","content":"`d, p, e`","locale":"en"},"4548":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus-24","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus#definition-49--fiber","content":"from the component","locale":"en"},"4549":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus-25","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus#definition-49--fiber","content":"`π`","locale":"en"},"4550":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus-26","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus#definition-49--fiber","content":"parent fiber name, or `root`","locale":"en"},"4551":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus-27","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus#definition-49--fiber","content":"`σ`","locale":"en"},"4552":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus-28","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus#definition-49--fiber","content":"the fiber's **own** coeffect table (empty until it activates)","locale":"en"},"4553":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus-29","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus#definition-49--fiber","content":"`τ`","locale":"en"},"4554":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus-30","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus#definition-49--fiber","content":"retirement flag (`⊥` fresh, `⊤` once the orchestrator has retired it)","locale":"en"},"4555":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus-31","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus#definition-49--fiber","content":"`θ`","locale":"en"},"4556":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus-32","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus#definition-49--fiber","content":"lifecycle state","locale":"en"},"4557":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus-33","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus#definition-49--fiber","content":"`installed_n(γ) := θ_n ≠ Inactive`. The settled states are `Inactive` (contributes nothing) and `Active` (contributes its effects). `Reloading` and `Unloading` exist because a real transition takes an interval of time. The **committed view `ω` maps each declared key to the *name of the fiber that provided it* when the transition committed**.","locale":"en"},"4558":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus-34","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus#definition-50--registry","content":"A state carries `F_γ : 𝔑 ⇀ 𝔉Γ` whose parent pointers form a tree rooted at `root`. **Names are atoms**: they are never computed or inspected, only compared for equality; a new fiber draws a name not in use (generative names).","locale":"en"},"4559":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus-35","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus#definition-50--registry","content":"The coeffect context is **derived, not stored**:","locale":"en"},"4560":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus-36","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus#definition-50--registry","content":"This is well defined because `dom(σ_n) ⊆ p_n` and the provisions of distinct fibers are disjoint (O-Insert enforces it). Each key thus has at most one possible provider, `provider_k(γ)`.","locale":"en"},"4561":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus-37","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus#definition-50--registry","content":"Important consequences:","locale":"en"},"4562":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus-38","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus#definition-50--registry","content":"Only **Active** fibers provide. A `Reloading` fiber has written bindings into its own table, but they are not yet visible to dependents; an `Unloading` fiber has stopped providing even though its bindings are still in place. This is what allows a provider to *cease providing before it withdraws anything*.","locale":"en"},"4563":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus-39","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus#definition-50--registry","content":"A calculus with realms would relax disjointness to \"disjoint within a realm\" (§4.4, see Metatheory). The single shared realm restricts components with a non-empty provision to one fiber at a time.","locale":"en"},"4564":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus-40","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus#definition-51--registry-projection","content":"`σ^S_γ := ⋃ { σ_m|_S | m ∈ dom(F_γ) }`: the bindings at keys `S` over **every** fiber (not only Active ones). Witness conditions are read here, because a transition's own writes stay in the fiber's table before it is Active.","locale":"en"},"4565":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus-41","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus#the-nine-rules-42","content":"There are two relations: **orchestration** rules `γ ⇒ δ` (actions the orchestrator *may* perform; the premises say when they are legal, not when they occur) and **lifecycle** rules `γ ⟶ δ` (the system takes them unprompted whenever their premises hold). Any interleaving is a valid run.","locale":"en"},"4566":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus-42","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus#the-nine-rules-42","content":"O-Retire sets `τ` at any state and is omitted from the figure. The same figure in the paper's notation:","locale":"en"},"4567":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus-43","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus#orchestration-421","content":"Retiring is a **request**, and unconditional; the lifecycle rules carry it out. Retirement is separated from removal so that an Active retired fiber is deactivated first (removing it earlier would discard the accumulator and leak).","locale":"en"},"4568":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus-44","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus#orchestration-421","content":"O-Remove removes children before parents, and only entries with an empty table.","locale":"en"},"4569":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus-45","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus#orchestration-421","content":"The last premise of O-Insert is the **single-source discipline**.","locale":"en"},"4570":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus-46","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus#definition-52--instantiation","content":"An iteration of `e_n` may instantiate a component: it takes the O-Insert with `π = n` and **yields as its inverse the O-Retire** of the new fiber. The inverse *retires rather than removes* because an inverse must always be applicable, and O-Remove has premises (an Active child would block it). A retired, Inactive, empty entry is *vestigial* (Lemma 62, see Metatheory): it is indistinguishable from absence. Retiring a child does not make the parent wait; grandchildren are reached one level at a time.","locale":"en"},"4571":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus-47","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus#definition-53--target-view-and-quiescence","content":"The target depends only on retirement and coeffect resolution. **Recording a provider name rather than a value** is what makes the comparison usable: a different fiber providing an equal value would otherwise compare equal. (Implementation: `fiber.committed` holds the map, `fiber.target` a hash.)","locale":"en"},"4572":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus-48","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus#activation","content":"Each iteration composes its inverse as `g ∘ h`, so the accumulator applies inverses LIFO.","locale":"en"},"4573":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus-49","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus#deactivation","content":"A deactivation cannot be one step: a consumer torn down *because its provider is leaving* runs teardown code that may need that very provider (for example, returning connections to a pool). So the **decision** is separated from the **act**, and the act is guarded.","locale":"en"},"4574":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus-50","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus#deactivation","content":"**Definition 54 – relied upon:** `relied_n(γ) := ∃m ≠ n, k ∈ d_m. installed_m(γ) ∧ ω_m(k) = n`","locale":"en"},"4575":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus-51","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus#deactivation","content":"**L-Divert** may fall between any two iterations; it routes into Unloading with the inverses accumulated so far (not through Active, which would let dependents activate against a leaving component). The *abort* alternative needs an iteration boundary; the *landing* alternative lets the in-flight iteration complete (needed for asynchronous hosts, §4.4).","locale":"en"},"4576":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus-52","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus#deactivation","content":"**L-Leave** records the decision only: the fiber stops providing (its table leaves `σ_γ`) while its own committed view and everybody else's stay intact.","locale":"en"},"4577":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus-53","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus#deactivation","content":"**L-Unload** is the *only* rule that applies an accumulator; it discards the committed view last. Its premise `¬relied_n` is **the guard**: a provider's withdrawal waits until every consumer that resolved a key to it has gone.","locale":"en"},"4578":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus-54","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus#deactivation","content":"The guard is **per binding** (it tests committed views), and is ordered along coeffects, *not* along the fiber tree: a parent may run its inverse while its child is still Unloading.","locale":"en"},"4579":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus-55","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus#deactivation","content":"Why the guard does not deadlock: once a provider is marked (L-Leave or L-Divert) its table leaves `σ_γ`, so no target view can name it any longer, and every consumer committed to it is itself on its way out (Theorem 73, see Metatheory).","locale":"en"},"4580":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus-56","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus#deactivation","content":"The rules are nondeterministic and mention no scheduler: theorems over all sequences hold for every scheduling policy.","locale":"en"},"4581":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus-57","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus#deactivation","content":"**In kardia:** a fiber's target is `Target::Inactive` (`⊥`) or `Target::Epoch { revision, providers }`, the `ImplId`s of its providers in `inject` order; `ImplId` and `FiberId` are never reused. The committed view (`ω`) is a key → `ImplId` map fixed for the episode, and each binding keeps the set of fibers whose committed view names it (`relied_n`), which the guard waits on. In a plugin's `apply`, `ctx.checkpoint()` is an iteration boundary: it returns `Err(Interrupted)` once the load has been superseded or the fiber retired, which is L-Divert's abort alternative. See Lifecycle.","locale":"en"},"4582":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus-58","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus#confinement-423","content":"**Definition 55 – confined to `n`.** A map is confined to `n` if it (1) *writes* only `σ_n` and the values other tables hold at keys of `d_n` (a consumer operating on a coeffect writes the provider's table at that key), never control fields or the registry domain; and (2) *reads* only `σ_n` and `σ_m|_{d_n}`. Instantiation is the one exception (it writes a fresh entry; its inverse writes one `τ`).","locale":"en"},"4583":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus-59","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus#confinement-423","content":"**Definition 56 – context-mediated iterators for `n`.** The least set containing the unit and three iteration forms: the lift of an operation stage at a key of `d_n ∪ p_n`; the lift of a provision stage at a key of `p_n` (landing in `σ_n`); an instantiation. **Every fiber's effect function must lie in this set.**","locale":"en"},"4584":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus-60","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus#confinement-423","content":"**Lemma 57**: its members are confined to `n` and witnessed at `≃_{d_n ∪ p_n}`.","locale":"en"},"4585":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus-61","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus#confinement-423","content":"Practical meaning: a component may not branch on the lifecycle state of a fiber it did not declare, nor read or write tables outside its two declarations, nor anything no table holds.","locale":"en"},"4586":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus-62","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus#table-1--rules-as-writes","content":"Rule","locale":"en"},"4587":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus-63","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus#table-1--rules-as-writes","content":"`θ` before","locale":"en"},"4588":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus-64","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus#table-1--rules-as-writes","content":"`θ` after","locale":"en"},"4589":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus-65","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus#table-1--rules-as-writes","content":"State map `Ψ`","locale":"en"},"4590":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus-66","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-calculus#table-1--rules-as-writes","content":"Control fields edited","locale":"en"},"459