id":"/en/docs/concepts-91","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"Operations a value at key `k` offers; they induce the observational equivalence `≃_k`.","locale":"en"},"5219":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-92","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"Commutative key","locale":"en"},"5220":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-93","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"All operations at the key are pairwise independent; a *witnessed coeffect* carries that proof.","locale":"en"},"5221":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-94","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"Component `ℭΓ`","locale":"en"},"5222":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-95","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"Triple `(d, p, e)`.","locale":"en"},"5223":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-96","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"Fiber","locale":"en"},"5224":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-97","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"Instantiation `⟨d, p, e, π, σ, τ, θ⟩` (parent, own table, retirement flag, lifecycle state).","locale":"en"},"5225":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-98","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"Lifecycle state `θ`","locale":"en"},"5226":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-99","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"`Inactive \\| Reloading(i,g,ω) \\| Active(g,ω) \\| Unloading(g,ω)`.","locale":"en"},"5227":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-100","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"Committed view `ω`","locale":"en"},"5228":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-101","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"Map from declared keys to the *names of providing fibers* at commit time.","locale":"en"},"5229":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-102","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"Target view","locale":"en"},"5230":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-103","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"What `ω` should be now (`⊥` if retired or unsatisfied).","locale":"en"},"5231":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-104","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"Guard (`relied_n`)","locale":"en"},"5232":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-105","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"L-Unload may fire only when no installed fiber's committed view names `n`.","locale":"en"},"5233":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-106","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"Quiescent","locale":"en"},"5234":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-107","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"Every fiber settled at its target.","locale":"en"},"5235":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-108","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"Support set `A`","locale":"en"},"5236":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-109","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"Fibers that are not retired, whose parent is supported, and whose declared keys are provided by supported fibers.","locale":"en"},"5237":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-110","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"Inertia","locale":"en"},"5238":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-111","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"Asynchronous hosts cannot abort an iteration in flight; only the *landing* alternative of L-Divert is available.","locale":"en"},"5239":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-112","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"System boundary","locale":"en"},"5240":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-113","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"Locations the system can exclusively modify and restore lie inside; others lie outside (emissions cross it).","locale":"en"},"5241":{"id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency","page_id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency","type":"page","content":"Concurrency and execution model","breadcrumbs":["kardia Documentation","Development"],"tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-concurrency","locale":"en"},"5242":{"id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency-0","page_id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-concurrency","content":"How kardia reproduces JavaScript's run-to-completion atomicity with one lock per root, critical sections that return actions, and spawned transitions.","locale":"en"},"5243":{"id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency-1","page_id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-concurrency#why","content":"Why the model matters","locale":"en"},"5244":{"id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency-2","page_id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-concurrency#model","content":"Chosen model","locale":"en"},"5245":{"id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency-3","page_id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-concurrency#lock-ordering","content":"Lock ordering","locale":"en"},"5246":{"id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency-4","page_id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-concurrency","content":"This page is decision **D1** of the guiding decisions: a `Send + Sync` core with one state lock per root, in which no user code runs while the lock is held.","locale":"en"},"5247":{"id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency-5","page_id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-concurrency#why","content":"Cordis's correctness rests on JavaScript's run-to-completion semantics: code between two `await`s is atomic. Many algorithms (inertia in `_setEpoch`, `_initTask ??=` in the loader, \"assign options synchronously then commit\") depend on this. A Rust port must reproduce *that atomicity*, not JS's single-threadedness per se.","locale":"en"},"5248":{"id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency-6","page_id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-concurrency#model","content":"All mutable runtime state of one root lives in `Root { state: parking_lot::Mutex<State>, … }`.","locale":"en"},"5249":{"id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency-7","page_id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-concurrency#model","content":"**Porting rule:** every synchronous segment of the TS code becomes **one critical section** on `State`. Nothing awaits while holding the lock, and **no user code is called while holding the lock** (plugin `apply`, disposers, event listeners, availability checks).","locale":"en"},"5250":{"id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency-8","page_id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-concurrency#model","content":"Critical sections are written as functions `fn step(&mut State, …) -> Actions`, where `Actions` is a small list of deferred work: `Spawn(Transition)`, `Emit(InternalEvent)`, `Wake(Notify)`, `RunDisposers(Vec<Disposer>)`. A tiny driver executes the actions after releasing the lock. This keeps the state machine **pure and unit-testable** and removes the reentrancy hazards JS hides (in TS, `_updateState` synchronously emits `internal/status` to user listeners from inside the state machine).","locale":"en"},"5251":{"id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency-9","page_id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-concurrency#model","content":"Transitions (`reload`, `unload`) are spawned tokio tasks. The paper's footnote 2 anticipates this: *\"with lazy scheduling (e.g. … Rust futures) the host must spawn the task for it to progress.\"*","locale":"en"},"5252":{"id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency-10","page_id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-concurrency#model","content":"Handles (`Context`, `Fiber`, `Svc<S>`) are `Send + Sync`; plugin futures must be `Send`. Users can freely `tokio::spawn` inside plugins (and tie the `JoinHandle` to an effect).","locale":"en"},"5253":{"id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency-11","page_id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-concurrency#model","content":"Tests run on a `current_thread` runtime with `tokio::time::pause()` for deterministic interleavings (the analogue of vitest fake timers); production may use the multi-threaded runtime. Testing describes which runtime each test suite uses today.","locale":"en"},"5254":{"id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency-12","page_id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-concurrency#model","content":"In `kardia-core` (`root.rs`), the root is the private `RootInner { state: Mutex<State>, changed: Notify, logger: Mutex<LoggerState> }`. A critical section is `RootInner::run(|st: &mut State| …)`: it pushes `Action`s onto `State::actions` instead of returning them. The actions are `Reload(FiberId)` and `Unload(FiberId)` (spawn the transition), `Emit(..)` (deliver an event to listeners), `Log { .. }` (report an error through the logger) and `Drop(..)` (values released under the lock are dropped after it, since their destructors are user code). Instead of per-action `Wake(Notify)`, every critical section wakes all waiters on the root-wide `changed` notifier after unlocking. Disposers are not run as an action: the unload transition runs them itself, outside the lock (see Lifecycle algorithms).","locale":"en"},"5255":{"id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency-13","page_id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-concurrency#model","content":"**Alternative considered:** a `!Send` design (`Rc<RefCell<..>>` on a `LocalSet`) mirrors JS most literally and was recommended by one analysis pass for the loader. It is rejected for the core because it would force every plugin and service in the ecosystem to be `!Send`-compatible (no `tokio::spawn`, awkward with most async libraries), while the lock-plus-actions discipline above preserves the same atomicity with comparable complexity. If profiling later shows lock contention on the access hot path, committed views (immutable per episode) can be published through `arc-swap` without changing semantics.","locale":"en"},"5256":{"id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency-14","page_id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-concurrency#lock-ordering","content":"`core::State` is the innermost lock. Higher layers (`loader` tree state, `include` IO state) have their own mutexes and may call into core only **after releasing** their own lock or while holding it **only if core never calls back into them under its lock** (which D1 guarantees).","locale":"en"},"5257":{"id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency-15","page_id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-concurrency#lock-ordering","content":"Rule of thumb: *take at most one lock at a time*.","locale":"en"},"5258":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","type":"page","content":"Contributing","breadcrumbs":["kardia Documentation","Development"],"tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-contributing","locale":"en"},"5259":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-0","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing","content":"How to work on kardia: building and testing, the xtask commands, CI, releasing to crates.io and npm, and the documentation site.","locale":"en"},"5260":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-1","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#failure-isolation","content":"Mandatory failure isolation and configurable recovery","locale":"en"},"5261":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-2","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#building","content":"Building and testing","locale":"en"},"5262":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-3","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#js","content":"The npm packages","locale":"en"},"5263":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-4","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#xtask","content":"Repository tasks: `cargo xtask`","locale":"en"},"5264":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-5","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"CI","locale":"en"},"5265":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-6","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#releases","content":"Releases","locale":"en"},"5266":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-7","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#npm-releases","content":"npm releases","locale":"en"},"5267":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-8","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#npm-setup","content":"One-time setup","locale":"en"},"5268":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-9","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#credentials","content":"Secrets, variables and credentials","locale":"en"},"5269":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-10","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#site","content":"Documentation site","locale":"en"},"5270":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-11","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#content","content":"Content","locale":"en"},"5271":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-12","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#deployment","content":"Deployment","locale":"en"},"5272":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-13","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing","content":"kardia is developed at github.com/licyk/kardia. This page covers the Rust workspace, the npm packages under `js/`, their CI and releases, and this documentation site.","locale":"en"},"5273":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-14","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing","content":"The repository's `AGENTS.md` has the code rules. In short: breaking refactors are allowed, with no compatibility layers; validate external data once, at the boundary, and tolerate unknown fields; no complexity for speculative races or edge cases without agreement first; keep main functions on the normal flow, with details in small helpers below; comment only non-obvious constraints; regenerate generated files instead of editing them; test the real implementation with few mocks; follow the repository's existing tools and workflows; extract a function only when it is reused, complex or a nameable concept.","locale":"en"},"5274":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-15","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#failure-isolation","content":"`AGENTS.md` makes these acceptance requirements for plugin implementations and refactoring:","locale":"en"},"5275":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-16","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#failure-isolation","content":"Plugin errors, unwind panics, failed background tasks, and isolated guest crashes must not terminate the host. Only the failed plugin and consumers of its unavailable services may be interrupted; unrelated plugins must continue serving requests and making progress throughout recovery.","locale":"en"},"5276":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-17","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#failure-isolation","content":"Users must be able to select automatic retry, remaining failed until manual intervention, or host-defined handling. Do not force automatic recovery or restart; document the default and honor the selected policy. Unrelated plugins must keep working under every policy.","locale":"en"},"5277":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-18","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#failure-isolation","content":"When automatic retry is selected, supervision must outlive the failed instance and retry at a bounded rate. Disabling, removing, or disposing the plugin cancels pending recovery. Report invalid configuration instead of repeatedly retrying it unchanged. Manual mode must not retry on its own; host-defined mode reports the failure to the host, follows its decision, and contains failures in the handler itself.","locale":"en"},"5278":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-19","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#failure-isolation","content":"Distinguish a failed request or connection from a failed instance. Withdraw a failed instance's services and clean up its routes, listeners, connections, streams, and background tasks before replacing it. Do not reset the root context, duplicate registrations, or leave stale tasks running. User callbacks and asynchronous cleanup run outside runtime locks.","locale":"en"},"5279":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-20","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#failure-isolation","content":"Inject faults into the real implementation and verify all three policies: automatic retry without test code calling recovery APIs, remaining failed until explicit handling in manual mode, and following the host's decision while containing handler failures in custom mode. Continuously check unrelated plugins under every policy, repeat failures to verify cleanup, and verify that disposal cancels pending recovery. Validate the relevant native platforms separately.","locale":"en"},"5280":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-21","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#failure-isolation","content":"`Fiber::set_recovery` now selects `Manual` (default), bounded `Retry`, or `Host` handling. The supervisor outlives the failed instance, waits for cleanup and cancels on disposal or policy changes. Loader entries expose the same choice through `recovery`; see configuration and examples and native fault evidence. `restart()` still does not clear failure; manual handling uses `update`, while selected supervision drives the supported recovery operation. Unrelated plugins must remain operational under every policy. In-process unwind handling cannot contain abort, segfault or process-wide termination. Use existing process/WASM boundaries where needed; native dylibs do not provide crash isolation.","locale":"en"},"5281":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-22","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#building","content":"The WASM guest test builds its fixture as a WebAssembly module, so install the target first: `rustup target add wasm32-unknown-unknown`.","locale":"en"},"5282":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-23","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#building","content":"Some tests build fixture crates with cargo while they run, so the first run takes a little longer.","locale":"en"},"5283":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-24","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#building","content":"The minimum supported Rust version of the crates is 1.85 (`rust-version` in the workspace `Cargo.toml`); CI uses a pinned, newer toolchain (below).","locale":"en"},"5284":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-25","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#building","content":"How the tests are organised, and which runtime each suite uses, is on Testing.","locale":"en"},"5285":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-26","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#js","content":"`js/` is a Bun workspace of the `@kardiajs/*` packages (TypeScript and Tauri), outside the Cargo workspace:","locale":"en"},"5286":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-27","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#js","content":"The packages import each other by name. Type checking and tests resolve those names to the sources (`tsconfig.json` paths, `vitest.config.ts` aliases), so they need no build first; the build resolves them to the built packages.","locale":"en"},"5287":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-28","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#js","content":"Inside a package, modules import each other by absolute path: `import { Fiber } from \"#/fiber\"`. `#/` is a subpath import, mapped in each `package.json` (`\"imports\": { \"#/*\": { \"source\": \"./src/*.ts\", \"types\": \"./lib/*.d.ts\", \"default\": \"./lib/*.js\" } }`). Node, TypeScript and bundlers resolve it per package, so the published `lib/` works as `tsc` emits it; type checking and tests resolve it to the sources through the `source` condition (`customConditions` in `tsconfig.json`, `ssr.resolve.conditions` in `vitest.config.ts`). The `@/` alias of `site/` would not survive into a published package, since `tsc` does not rewrite path aliases.","locale":"en"},"5288":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-29","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#js","content":"The build copies the repository's `LICENSE` and `js/NOTICE` (the MIT notice of Cordis) into each package, since npm only ships files inside a package directory; the copies are gitignored.","locale":"en"},"5289":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-30","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#js","content":"Packages require each other as peers (`\"@kardiajs/core\": \"^0.1\"`), so an app has one copy of the runtime.","locale":"en"},"5290":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-31","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#xtask","content":"The `xtask` crate (`xtask/`, a workspace member that is never published) holds the repository's own tasks; `.cargo/config.toml` makes them `cargo xtask <command>`.","locale":"en"},"5291":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-32","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#xtask","content":"Command","locale":"en"},"5292":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-33","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#xtask","content":"What it does","locale":"en"},"5293":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-34","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#xtask","content":"`cargo xtask arch`","locale":"en"},"5294":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-35","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#xtask","content":"Checks the module structure: every `lib.rs` and `mod.rs` (in `crates/*/src`, `crates/*/tests` and `xtask/src`) only declares modules, re-exports, and holds docs and attributes. Implementation (functions, types, traits, `impl` blocks, constants, statics, `macro_rules!`, inline test modules) lives in a file of its own, re-exported from the entry file. Inline modules that only re-export (such as `kardia::prelude`) and proc-macro entry points, which Rust requires at the crate root, are allowed. It lists each violation with its file, line and kind, and fails if there is any. CI runs it.","locale":"en"},"5295":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-36","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#xtask","content":"`cargo xtask publish-check`","locale":"en"},"5296":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-37","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#xtask","content":"Offline cycle check for local path dependencies between publishable workspace crates. Includes normal/build dependencies and dev-dependencies with a version (including workspace inheritance); omits path-only dev-dependencies. Reports the first cycle with manifest paths, dependency keys, target conditions and optional flags. Exits nonzero on a cycle.","locale":"en"},"5297":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-38","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#xtask","content":"`cargo xtask version <patch\\|minor\\|major> [--dry-run]`","locale":"en"},"5298":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-39","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#xtask","content":"Bumps the workspace version (`0.1.3` → `0.1.4` / `0.2.0` / `1.0.0`) in `[workspace.package]`, in the `kardia-*` entries of `[workspace.dependencies]`, and in `Cargo.lock`. When the requirement users write changes (a minor or major bump before 1.0, a major bump after), it also updates the `kardia = \"0.x\"` snippets of the README and these docs. It does not commit.","locale":"en"},"5299":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-40","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#xtask","content":"`cargo xtask tag [--version X.Y.Z \\| --name NAME] [--message TEXT] [--push] [--remote NAME] [--dry-run]`","locale":"en"},"5300":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-41","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#xtask","content":"Creates an annotated tag on `HEAD`: `v<workspace version>` by default. `--version` must equal the workspace version (a `v*` tag releases it), and `--name` creates any other tag. It refuses an uncommitted working tree and a tag that already exists, locally or (with `--push`) on the remote. `--push` pushes the tag (to `origin`, or `--remote`).","locale":"en"},"5301":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-42","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#xtask","content":"`cargo xtask js-version <patch\\|minor\\|major> [--dry-run]`","locale":"en"},"5302":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-43","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#xtask","content":"Bumps the version of the npm packages, which share one version of their own: `version` in each `js/packages/*/package.json`, the `^` ranges they require of each other when the requirement changes, and `js/bun.lock` (`bun install`). It refuses packages at different versions. It does not commit.","locale":"en"},"5303":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-44","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#xtask","content":"`cargo xtask js-tag [same options as tag]`","locale":"en"},"5304":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-45","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#xtask","content":"Like `tag`, for the npm packages: `js-v<version>` by default, which starts the npm release.","locale":"en"},"5305":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-46","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#xtask","content":"`publish-check` runs `cargo metadata --no-deps --offline --locked` and reads the original TOML declarations. An explicit `version = \"*\"` remains a published dev-dependency; omitting `version` does not. Target-specific and optional dependencies are checked even when inactive on the host. This is a manual local command: it does not change manifests or lockfiles, package crates, compile application code, query registries, or run automatically from the release/tag workflows. It checks workspace publishing cycles, not external registry dependencies or every possible packaging failure.","locale":"en"},"5306":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-47","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"The repository has five workflows, each for one part of it, so that a change runs only the checks it needs and a release tests a commit once.","locale":"en"},"5307":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-48","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"Workflow","locale":"en"},"5308":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-49","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"Runs on","locale":"en"},"5309":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-50","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"Does","locale":"en"},"5310":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-51","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"`ci.yml` (Rust)","locale":"en"},"5311":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-52","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"pushes to `main` and pull requests, except changes only to `site/`, `js/`, Markdown, `LICENSE`, `NOTICE` and the hosting files; manual runs; called by Release","locale":"en"},"5312":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-53","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"fmt, clippy, `cargo xtask arch`, the test suite","locale":"en"},"5313":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-54","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"`js.yml` (npm packages)","locale":"en"},"5314":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-55","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"pushes to `main` and pull requests that change `js/`, `crates/kardia-core/` or `crates/kardia-bridge/`; manual runs; called by npm release","locale":"en"},"5315":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-56","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"package types, tests and builds on Linux, Windows and macOS; end-to-end recovery tests against the real Rust host","locale":"en"},"5316":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-57","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"`site.yml` (docs)","locale":"en"},"5317":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-58","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"pushes to `main` and pull requests that change `site/`; manual runs","locale":"en"},"5318":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-59","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"the site's types, tests and build; outside pull requests, the deploy (below)","locale":"en"},"5319":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-60","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"`release.yml`","locale":"en"},"5320":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-61","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"`v*` tags; manual runs","locale":"en"},"5321":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-62","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"publishes to crates.io (below)","locale":"en"},"5322":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-63","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"`npm-release.yml`","locale":"en"},"5323":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-64","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"`js-v*` tags (which `v*` does not match); manual runs","locale":"en"},"5324":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-65","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"publishes to npm (below)","locale":"en"},"5325":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-66","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"CI's steps:","locale":"en"},"5326":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-67","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"Check out the repository.","locale":"en"},"5327":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-68","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"Install Rust **1.99.1** (`dtolnay/rust-toolchain@1.99.1`) with `rustfmt`, `clippy` and the `wasm32-unknown-unknown` target. The toolchain is pinned because the compile-error snapshots of `kardia-macros` (trybuild tests) depend on the compiler version.","locale":"en"},"5328":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-69","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"Restore the cargo cache (`Swatinem/rust-cache`).","locale":"en"},"5329":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-70","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"`cargo fmt --all --check`","locale":"en"},"5330":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-71","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"`cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings`","locale":"en"},"5331":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-72","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"`cargo xtask arch`","locale":"en"},"5332":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-73","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"`cargo test --workspace`","locale":"en"},"5333":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-74","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"A newer push cancels an older CI run of the same branch or pull request. Tags never start CI.","locale":"en"},"5334":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-75","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"A change to","locale":"en"},"5335":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-76","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"Runs","locale":"en"},"5336":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-77","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"a docs page (`site/`)","locale":"en"},"5337":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-78","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"`site.yml` only","locale":"en"},"5338":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-79","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"the README, `AGENTS.md`, the licence","locale":"en"},"5339":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-80","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"nothing","locale":"en"},"5340":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-81","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"Rust code, tests, `Cargo.*`, `xtask/`, a workflow other than `site.yml` and the js ones","locale":"en"},"5341":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-82","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"`ci.yml`","locale":"en"},"5342":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-83","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"`js/`","locale":"en"},"5343":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-84","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"`js.yml` only","locale":"en"},"5344":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-85","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"`crates/kardia-core/` or `crates/kardia-bridge/`","locale":"en"},"5345":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-86","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"`ci.yml` and `js.yml`","locale":"en"},"5346":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-87","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"a version bump, then its tag","locale":"en"},"5347":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-88","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"`ci.yml` once; `release.yml` reuses that run","locale":"en"},"5348":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-89","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"an npm version bump, then its `js-v*` tag","locale":"en"},"5349":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-90","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"`js.yml` on the push, and again as the release's checks","locale":"en"},"5350":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-91","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#releases","content":"All crates share one version, `version` under `[workspace.package]` in the root `Cargo.toml` (the `[workspace.dependencies]` entries of the kardia crates carry it too).","locale":"en"},"5351":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-92","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#releases","content":"`cargo xtask version patch` (or `minor`, `major`), then commit and push: `git commit -am \"chore(release): kardia 0.1.1\"`, `git push`. The push runs CI.","locale":"en"},"5352":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-93","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#releases","content":"`cargo xtask tag --push` tags the commit `v0.1.1` and pushes the tag, which starts `.github/workflows/release.yml`.","locale":"en"},"5353":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-94","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#releases","content":"Release checks that the tag names the workspace version. It then finds CI's run of the tagged commit and waits for it if it is still running: a passed run is reused, a failed one stops the release. Only when there is no usable run (a commit CI never ran on, or a run a newer push cancelled) does Release call `ci.yml` itself as its checks. Each released commit is tested once.","locale":"en"},"5354":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-95","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#releases","content":"It publishes every crate whose new version is not on crates.io yet, in dependency order (`cargo publish --workspace`; `xtask` is never published), then creates a GitHub release for the tag, with notes since the previous `v*` tag.","locale":"en"},"5355":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-96","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#releases","content":"The publish step is **resumable**:","locale":"en"},"5356":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-97","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#releases","content":"Before each attempt, it asks the crates.io API which `name@version` pairs already exist and excludes those crates, so a crate is never published twice.","locale":"en"},"5357":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-98","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#releases","content":"crates.io lets an account create only a few new crates at once and then one every ten minutes. When `cargo publish` fails with `429 Too Many Requests`, the workflow waits 610 seconds and tries again, up to eight attempts; any other failure fails the step. A release that adds new crates waits out this limit.","locale":"en"},"5358":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-99","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#releases","content":"The workflow can also be started by hand (`workflow_dispatch`) on a branch to resume a release that stopped part-way.","locale":"en"},"5359":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-100","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#releases","content":"It publishes with the `CARGO_REGISTRY_TOKEN` repository secret.","locale":"en"},"5360":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-101","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#npm-releases","content":"The `@kardiajs/*` packages share one version, apart from the crates' (they start at 0.1.0). The bridge's protocol has a version of its own (`PROTOCOL` on both sides): a client opening a session with another version is refused with an `invalid` error, so a host and a client from different releases fail clearly instead of misbehaving.","locale":"en"},"5361":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-102","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#npm-releases","content":"`cargo xtask js-version patch` (or `minor`, `major`), then commit and push: `git commit -am \"chore(release): @kardiajs 0.1.1\"`, `git push`.","locale":"en"},"5362":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-103","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#npm-releases","content":"`cargo xtask js-tag --push` tags the commit `js-v0.1.1` and pushes the tag, which starts `.github/workflows/npm-release.yml`.","locale":"en"},"5363":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-104","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#npm-releases","content":"The workflow checks that every package is at the tagged version, runs `js.yml` as its checks, builds the packages, and publishes core, timer, bridge, tauri and vue, in that order, skipping a version npm already has (so a manual run resumes a release that stopped part-way). It then creates a GitHub release for the tag, with notes since the previous `js-v*` tag.","locale":"en"},"5364":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-105","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#npm-releases","content":"It publishes with **npm trusted publishing**: the publish job's `id-token: write` permission lets it mint a short-lived OIDC token that npm accepts for packages whose trusted publisher names this repository and workflow. No npm token is stored anywhere, and npm attaches a provenance statement to each version automatically (this needs the repository to stay public).","locale":"en"},"5365":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-106","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#npm-setup","content":"**npmjs.com, organization.** Create the free organization `kardiajs` (the `kardia` package name and the `@kardia` scope belong to others), and add the maintainers' accounts. Enable two-factor authentication on them.","locale":"en"},"5366":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-107","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#npm-setup","content":"**npmjs.com, first publish.** npm configures a trusted publisher in a package's settings, so each package must exist first. From a machine logged in with `npm login`: `cd js && bun install && bun run build`, then `npm publish --access public` in `packages/core`, `timer`, `bridge`, `tauri` and `vue`, in that order. This publishes the first version without provenance; later versions come from CI. A package added later, such as `@kardiajs/vue`, needs this step, and its own trusted publisher, before its first CI release.","locale":"en"},"5367":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-108","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#npm-setup","content":"**GitHub, environment.** In the repository's *Settings → Environments*, create `npm`. Optionally restrict its deployments to `js-v*` tags and add required reviewers: the publish job waits for them.","locale":"en"},"5368":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-109","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#npm-setup","content":"**npmjs.com, trusted publishers.** In each package's *Settings → Trusted publishing*, add a GitHub Actions publisher: organization or user `licyk`, repository `kardia`, workflow `npm-release.yml`, environment `npm`. The fields are case-sensitive and npm checks them only at publish time. A new trusted publisher must publish within two days, or it expires and has to be recreated, so release a version (step 5) right after.","locale":"en"},"5369":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-110","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#npm-setup","content":"**First CI release.** Bump and tag as above. When it has published, set each package's *Publishing access* to \"Require two-factor authentication and disallow tokens\", so only the trusted publisher (and maintainers with 2FA) can publish.","locale":"en"},"5370":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-111","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#credentials","content":"Where","locale":"en"},"5371":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-112","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#credentials","content":"Name","locale":"en"},"5372":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-113","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#credentials","content":"Used by","locale":"en"},"5373":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-114","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#credentials","content":"Notes","locale":"en"},"5374":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-115","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#credentials","content":"GitHub, repository secret","locale":"en"},"5375":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-116","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#credentials","content":"`CARGO_REGISTRY_TOKEN`","locale":"en"},"5376":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-117","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#credentials","content":"`release.yml`","locale":"en"},"5377":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-118","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#credentials","content":"crates.io API token with the publish scopes; existing","locale":"en"},"5378":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-119","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#credentials","content":"GitHub, repository secret","locale":"en"},"5379":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-120","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#credentials","content":"`GITEE_RSA_PRIVATE_KEY`","locale":"en"},"5380":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-121","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#credentials","content":"`site.yml`","locale":"en"},"5381":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-122","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#credentials","content":"deploy key that pushes the site to `gh-pages`; existing","locale":"en"},"5382":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-123","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#credentials","content":"GitHub, environment","locale":"en"},"5383":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-124","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#credentials","content":"`npm`","locale":"en"},"5384":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-125","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#credentials","content":"`npm-release.yml` (publish job)","locale":"en"},"5385":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-126","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#credentials","content":"holds no secret: it names the trusted publisher's environment and carries any protection rules","locale":"en"},"5386":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-127","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#credentials","content":"GitHub, repository variables","locale":"en"},"5387":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-128","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#credentials","content":"none","locale":"en"},"5388":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-129","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#credentials","content":"GitHub, workflow token","locale":"en"},"5389":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-130","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#credentials","content":"`GITHUB_TOKEN`","locale":"en"},"5390":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-131","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#credentials","content":"the release jobs","locale":"en"},"5391":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-132","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#credentials","content":"provided by Actions; the jobs ask for `contents: write` (GitHub release) and `id-token: write` (npm) only where needed","locale":"en"},"5392":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-133","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#credentials","content":"npmjs.com","locale":"en"},"5393":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-134","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#credentials","content":"organization `kardiajs`, a trusted publisher per package, the 2FA-and-no-tokens policy","locale":"en"},"5394":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-135","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#credentials","content":"npm","locale":"en"},"5395":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-136","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#credentials","content":"configured on npmjs.com, not in GitHub","locale":"en"},"5396":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-137","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#credentials","content":"There is no `NPM_TOKEN`. If trusted publishing ever has to be bypassed, a granular npm access token limited to the `@kardiajs` packages, stored as an `npm` environment secret, would be the fallback; the workflow does not use one.","locale":"en"},"5397":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-138","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#site","content":"This site lives in `site/`: Fumadocs on TanStack Start (React), built with Vite and Tailwind CSS, with Bun as the package manager and script runner. It is outside the Cargo workspace, so `cargo` commands are unaffected.","locale":"en"},"5398":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-139","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#site","content":"`bun.lock` is committed; `bun install --frozen-lockfile` installs exactly the locked versions, as the deployment workflow does.","locale":"en"},"5399":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-140","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#content","content":"Pages are MDX files in `site/content/docs/`, with flat slugs grouped by prefix (`guide-*`, `concepts-*`, `reference-*`, `development-*`).","locale":"en"},"5400":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-141","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#content","content":"The site is bilingual. Chinese is the default language: `<slug>.mdx`, served without a prefix. English is `<slug>.en.mdx`. Both languages use the same page URL, selected from browser language or the user’s saved preference. Every page exists in both languages, and a test checks it.","locale":"en"},"5401":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-142","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#content","content":"The sidebar order and its section headings come from `meta.json` (Chinese) and `meta.en.json` (English). Both must list the same slugs in the same order; a test checks that too.","locale":"en"},"5402":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-143","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#content","content":"Links between pages use `/docs/<slug>` (the site loads content in the selected language). Besides Fumadocs' defaults, pages can use the `Cards`, `Callout`, `Steps`, `Tabs`, `Accordions` and `Mermaid` components without imports.","locale":"en"},"5403":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-144","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#content","content":"Code, identifiers, configuration examples and the paper's notation stay as they are in both languages.","locale":"en"},"5404":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-145","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#deployment","content":"`.github/workflows/site.yml` checks and deploys the site; Rust's checks do not run for a docs-only change. A pull request that touches `site/` gets the types, tests and build. A push to `main` that touches `site/` also builds the site with `VITE_BASE_PATH=/kardia/` and publishes the output to the `gh-pages` branch (with the `GITEE_RSA_PRIVATE_KEY` deploy key), which GitHub Pages serves at **https\\://licyk.github.io/kardia/**. Deploys run one at a time.","locale":"en"},"5405":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-146","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#deployment","content":"The base path is only for GitHub Pages, which serves the site under `/kardia/`; without `VITE_BASE_PATH`, the build serves from `/`.","locale":"en"},"5406":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","type":"page","content":"API design","breadcrumbs":["kardia Documentation","Development"],"tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-design","locale":"en"},"5407":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-0","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design","content":"The design of kardia's API: services, keys and checked access, caller attribution, effects and interruption, plugins and configuration, the lifecycle state machine, events, the logger and the built-in plugin crates.","locale":"en"},"5408":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-1","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-design#services","content":"Services, keys and access","locale":"en"},"5409":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-2","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-design#service-trait","content":"The `Service` trait","locale":"en"},"5410":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-3","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-design#providing","content":"Providing","locale":"en"},"5411":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-4","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-design#accessing","content":"Accessing (Algorithm 6)","locale":"en"},"5412":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-5","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-design#caller-attribution","content":"Caller attribution (D4)","locale":"en"},"5413":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-6","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-design#mixins","content":"Associated properties and mixins","locale":"en"},"5414":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-7","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-design#effects","content":"Effects (D5, D6)","locale":"en"},"5415":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-8","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-design#effects-api","content":"API","locale":"en"},"5416":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-9","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-design#effects-semantics","content":"Semantics (from `fiber.ts:292-357`)","locale":"en"},"5417":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-10","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-design#interruption","content":"Interruption and cancellation safety (D6)","locale":"en"},"5418":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-11","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-design#plugins","content":"Plugins, configuration and the lifecycle","locale":"en"},"5419":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-12","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-design#plugin-trait","content":"Plugin trait","locale":"en"},"5420":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-13","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-design#configuration","content":"Configuration (D7)","locale":"en"},"5421":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-14","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-design#lifecycle","content":"The lifecycle state machine (`lifecycle.rs`)","locale":"en"},"5422":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-15","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-design#instantiation","content":"Instantiation (O-Insert / O-Retire)","locale":"en"},"5423":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-16","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-design#replace","content":"Registry replace","locale":"en"},"5424":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-17","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-design#fiber-handle","content":"`Fiber` handle API","locale":"en"},"5425":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-18","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-design#events","content":"Events, logger and built-in services","locale":"en"},"5426":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-19","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-design#typed-events","content":"Typed events","locale":"en"},"5427":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-20","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-design#logger","content":"Logger","locale":"en"},"5428":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-21","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-design#built-ins","content":"Built-in plugin crates","locale":"en"},"5429":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-22","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design","content":"This page covers the public API of `kardia-core` and the built-in plugin crates: services and access (decisions D3, D4), effects (D5, D6), plugins, configuration and the lifecycle (D7), and events, the logger and built-in services. The data structures these operate on are on Core data structures. For a user-oriented introduction, see the guide pages Plugins, Services and Effects.","locale":"en"},"5430":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-23","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design","content":"The signatures below are the plan's design. Most of them exist in the code under the same names; where a name, a parameter or a return type changed, a note under the sketch gives the current API. The deliberate behavioural departures are listed on Status and deviations.","locale":"en"},"5431":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-24","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#service-trait","content":"(Associated type defaults are not stable; the implementation will put `Meta` on a separate `trait Intercepted: Service` with a blanket \"no metadata\" default.)","locale":"en"},"5432":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-25","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#service-trait","content":"In the code there is neither a `Meta` type nor `Mergeable` / `Intercepted`. Interception metadata stays JSON: `trait Service: Any + Send + Sync { const NAME: &'static str; fn merge_intercept(values: Vec<Value>) -> Value { merge_shallow(values) } }`, where the values come outermost first and the default is a right-biased shallow merge of JSON objects. A service reads typed metadata with `ctx.intercept_meta::<T>(key)`. `#[derive(Service)]` (from `kardia-macros`) implements the trait; `#[service(name = \"database\")]` sets the key, which defaults to the hyphenated type name.","locale":"en"},"5433":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-26","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#providing","content":"Semantics follow `ReflectService.provide` exactly:","locale":"en"},"5434":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-27","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#providing","content":"a tracked effect;","locale":"en"},"5435":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-28","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#providing","content":"single source per realm (`AlreadyProvided { key, by }`);","locale":"en"},"5436":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-29","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#providing","content":"notify immediately only if the providing fiber is `Active`, otherwise on its transition to `Active`;","locale":"en"},"5437":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-30","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#providing","content":"the inverse removes the binding, notifies the realm, **waits until `dependents` is empty**, then removes the provision.","locale":"en"},"5438":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-31","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#providing","content":"In-place overwrite (`reflect.set`) is offered as `ctx.replace_value::<S>(..)` and, as in Cordis and the paper, is *not* observed by dependents.","locale":"en"},"5439":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-32","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#providing","content":"In the code, `provide` and `provide_arc` return `kardia_core::Result<EffectHandle>` (the error is `kardia_core::Error`, which wraps `KardiaError::AlreadyProvided` and the other variants). Further variants: `provide_as` / `provide_arc_as` (any value under an explicit key), `provide_checked` (with an availability predicate) and `provide_serialized_as` (a value with a JSON view for expressions and tooling). `ctx.replace_value` was not implemented: `KardiaError::NotProvided` and `ForeignBinding` are declared for it but no API returns them. Retiring a provider unbinds its live provisions at once (see Retired providers).","locale":"en"},"5440":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-33","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#accessing","content":"`get` implements `locate`:","locale":"en"},"5441":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-34","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#accessing","content":"own provisions in the same realm;","locale":"en"},"5442":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-35","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#accessing","content":"walk fibers upward;","locale":"en"},"5443":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-36","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#accessing","content":"stop at an isolation boundary;","locale":"en"},"5444":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-37","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#accessing","content":"the first fiber whose `committed` has the key returns it;","locale":"en"},"5445":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-38","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#accessing","content":"a fiber that declares the key without a committed impl → `Inactive`;","locale":"en"},"5446":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-39","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#accessing","content":"reaching the root → root has **unchecked** access to the live store (as in Cordis).","locale":"en"},"5447":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-40","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#accessing","content":"Reading the committed view (not the store) keeps a dependency readable during the consumer's own teardown (Theorem 70).","locale":"en"},"5448":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-41","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#accessing","content":"Typed declarations: `Plugin::Inject` can be a tuple of service types (`(Database, Cache)`), implemented with `macro_rules!` over tuples, producing both the runtime `InjectSpec` and a typed accessor `ctx.deps::<P::Inject>() -> Result<(Svc<Database>, Svc<Cache>)>`. This is the first, macro-free step towards the compile-time mediation the paper suggests (§5.1.4, §6.4).","locale":"en"},"5449":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-42","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#accessing","content":"In the code: `get` and `AccessError` are as sketched. `try_get_live` is `live::<S>()` (and `live_as::<T>(key)`); `get_dyn` is `get_as::<T>(key)` for a typed value under a name, and `view(key)` for the JSON view of a service, which the loader's expressions use. Typed declarations use the `Deps` trait (implemented with `macro_rules!` for tuples of up to eight services): `Inject::of::<(Database, Cache)>()` builds the runtime declaration, and `ctx.deps::<(Database, Cache)>()` returns `(Svc<Database>, Svc<Cache>)`. `Plugin` has no `Inject` associated type; it declares with `fn inject() -> Inject`. The compile-time step is `Injected<D>`, used by `#[plugin]` (see Checked typed access).","locale":"en"},"5450":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-43","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#caller-attribution","content":"Effects created *on behalf of the caller* use `svc.caller().effect(..)`: they land in the caller's fiber, exactly what Cordis's tracker achieves with `this.ctx`.","locale":"en"},"5451":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-44","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#caller-attribution","content":"A service resolves *its own* dependencies through the `Context` it stored at construction (its def site), so no shadow contexts are needed.","locale":"en"},"5452":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-45","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#caller-attribution","content":"Service crates expose caller-aware APIs as **extension traits on `Svc<S>`** (allowed by coherence because the trait is local):","locale":"en"},"5453":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-46","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#caller-attribution","content":"Interception metadata is resolved at access time per Def. 27 / `resolveIntercept`: `[declared inject meta, outer…, inner]` merged right-biased.","locale":"en"},"5454":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-47","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#caller-attribution","content":"In the code, `Svc<T>` holds the value `Arc<T>`, the caller `Context`, the `key`, the `impl_id` and the providing fiber, with `caller()`, `key()`, `impl_id()`, `arc()`, `provider()` and `logger()`. `intercept()` is `meta() -> Value` (merged with `S::merge_intercept`) and `intercepts() -> Vec<Value>` (the unmerged list, declared `inject` metadata first). The timer's `TimerExt::timeout` returns `Result<EffectHandle>` and registers `self.caller().effect_sync(\"ctx.timeout()\", …)`. Service-to-service calls follow the def site, unlike TS (see Service-to-service calls).","locale":"en"},"5455":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-48","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#mixins","content":"Cordis's `foo.bar` associated services and `ctx.mixin` are JS-object conveniences. In Rust: a service named `foo.bar` is just another key; \"extending another plugin's type\" is done with extension traits (compile time) or with a registry service built on `IdTable` (runtime). No accessor/mixin layer is ported.","locale":"en"},"5456":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-49","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#effects-api","content":"In the code (`effect.rs`, `fiber.rs`):","locale":"en"},"5457":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-50","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#effects-api","content":"`Disposer` is a struct consumed when it runs (`Disposer::new(FnOnce())`, `fallible(FnOnce() -> Result<()>)`, `future(..)`, `try_future(..)`); its closure runs synchronously when disposal starts and may return a future that finishes the work. Panics are caught and reported as errors.","locale":"en"},"5458":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-51","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#effects-api","content":"Labels are `impl Into<String>`. `effect_sync` takes `FnOnce(&EffectScope) -> Result<()>`: the body collects its inverses on the scope instead of returning one. `effect` takes `FnOnce(EffectScope) -> Fut` with `Fut: Future<Output = Result<()>>`; the body is spawned. Both return `kardia_core::Result<EffectHandle>`.","locale":"en"},"5459":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-52","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#effects-api","content":"`on_dispose` returns `Result<EffectHandle>`; `ctx.defer(label, disposer)` registers an arbitrary `Disposer`.","locale":"en"},"5460":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-53","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#effects-api","content":"`ctx.checkpoint()`, `scope.checkpoint()` and `scope.yield_(d)` are synchronous: `ctx.checkpoint()?`, `scope.yield_(d)?`. `EffectScope` methods take `&self` (the scope is `Clone`), and there is a `defer_fn` shorthand; `adopt` takes `impl Into<EffectHandle>` (a `Fiber` converts).","locale":"en"},"5461":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-54","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#effects-api","content":"`EffectHandle::dispose()` returns a `Disposal`, a future of `Result<()>` that can also be dropped; `ready()` returns a future of `Result<()>`. The handle also has `label()`, `is_active()` and `meta()`.","locale":"en"},"5462":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-55","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#effects-semantics","content":"`assert_active`: effects on a removed fiber fail with `KardiaError::InactiveEffect`.","locale":"en"},"5463":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-56","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#effects-semantics","content":"Inside one effect: disposers run **sequentially in reverse order**.","locale":"en"},"5464":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-57","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#effects-semantics","content":"Ownership transfer: a nested `EffectHandle` created inside an effect is registered on the fiber; `scope.adopt(h)` moves it under the outer effect (the TS `collect` → `_disposables.delete`).","locale":"en"},"5465":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-58","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#effects-semantics","content":"A failing effect body disposes what it collected; synchronous errors propagate to the caller, async ones are logged and reported via `ready()`.","locale":"en"},"5466":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-59","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#effects-semantics","content":"Fiber unload drains the accumulator in reverse insertion order and runs the disposers **concurrently** (`join_all`), logging failures and panics (`catch_unwind`), because Cordis tests require sibling effects to clean up concurrently (`service-dispose.spec.ts`). Ordered teardown is obtained by grouping steps in one effect.","locale":"en"},"5467":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-60","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#effects-semantics","content":"`EffectHandle` is a non-owning handle: dropping it does **not** dispose. Component lifetime is dynamic and governed by the fiber, which is exactly where the paper distinguishes Cordis from RAII (§7.3: lexical scoping is complementary, for resources local to one effect; inside a disposer, ordinary `Drop` types are fine).","locale":"en"},"5468":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-61","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#interruption","content":"Rust *can* cancel a future by dropping it, something JS cannot, so a Rust host could offer the paper's *aborting* L-Divert. The plan deliberately does **not** drop in-flight `apply` futures: a future dropped between \"acquire resource\" and \"register its disposer\" would leak exactly what temporal composability promises to recover. Instead:","locale":"en"},"5469":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-62","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#interruption","content":"the host is **inertial** (paper §4.4): a stale load lands, then unloads;","locale":"en"},"5470":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-63","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#interruption","content":"explicit boundaries, `scope.yield_(..).await?` and `ctx.checkpoint().await?`, return `Err(Interrupted)` when the fiber's target changed since the load began (the TS `while (runner.epoch === oldEpoch)` check). Returning `Interrupted` from `apply` is not a failure; the fiber unloads what it registered.","locale":"en"},"5471":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-64","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#interruption","content":"This is the loader's outer-fiber pattern (`yield () => {}` after import) made explicit.","locale":"en"},"5472":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-65","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#interruption","content":"The boundaries are synchronous in the code (`scope.yield_(..)?`, `ctx.checkpoint()?`). `ctx.checkpoint()` fails once the load has been superseded (the fiber's target no longer equals the target of its episode) or the fiber was retired; `scope.checkpoint()` fails once the effect has been disposed. Async effect bodies are spawned and never dropped half-way; disposal waits for the body to reach its end or a boundary.","locale":"en"},"5473":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-66","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#plugin-trait","content":"Erased form stored in runtimes: `PluginDef { key: RuntimeKey, name, inject: Arc<InjectSpec>, provide, decode: fn(&Value) -> Result<ErasedConfig, ValidationError>, apply: Arc<dyn Fn(Context, ErasedConfig) -> BoxFuture<'static, Result<(), PluginError>> + Send + Sync> }`. `RuntimeKey` is `TypeId::of::<P>()` for typed plugins and a pointer identity for closure plugins (dynamic modules add their own key kind, see Loader internals).","locale":"en"},"5474":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-67","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#plugin-trait","content":"In the code (`plugin.rs`, `registry.rs`):","locale":"en"},"5475":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-68","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#plugin-trait","content":"`Plugin` has `const NAME`, `type Config`, `fn inject() -> Inject` (default: empty), `fn provide() -> Vec<Key>` (default: empty) and `fn apply(ctx: Context, config: Arc<Self::Config>) -> impl Future<Output = Result<()>> + Send`. `Inject` (ordered keys, each with optional interception metadata) replaces `InjectSet` / `InjectSpec`.","locale":"en"},"5476":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-69","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#plugin-trait","content":"`ctx.plugin::<P>(config)`, `ctx.plugin_value::<P>(value)`, `ctx.plugin_def(&def, value)` and `ctx.inject(inject, f)` return `kardia_core::Result<Fiber>`; the closure of `inject` is `Fn(Context) -> Fut`. `ctx.plugin::<P>(config)` requires `P::Config: Serialize`: the typed config is serialized to a `Value` and decoded like any other configuration, so a `Deserialize`-only config is passed as JSON with `plugin_value`.","locale":"en"},"5477":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-70","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#plugin-trait","content":"`PluginDef` is a cheap clone of `Arc<{ key, name: Option<String>, inject, provide, tags, decode, apply }>`, built with `PluginDef::of::<P>()`, `PluginDef::new(apply)` or `PluginDef::typed(apply)` and refined with `.name(..)`, `.inject(..)`, `.provides(..)`, `.tag(..)`, `.validate_with(..)`. `RuntimeKey` is `Type(TypeId)` or `Def(u64)` (a counter-assigned identity for closure-based definitions). Dynamic modules reuse `Def`; they add no key kind of their own.","locale":"en"},"5478":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-71","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#configuration","content":"Configuration arrives as `serde_json::Value` (from files, the loader API, or `plugin_value`) and is decoded **once** per (re)load attempt with `serde_path_to_error`, producing `ValidationError { issues: Vec<Issue { path, message }> }`, the analogue of Standard Schema issues. Unknown fields are tolerated unless the plugin opts into `deny_unknown_fields` (repository rule: tolerate unknown fields).","locale":"en"},"5479":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-72","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#configuration","content":"`Fiber::update(value)`:","locale":"en"},"5480":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-73","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#configuration","content":"validate first (a rejected config leaves the running instance untouched);","locale":"en"},"5481":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-74","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#configuration","content":"emit `ConfigCommitted` (loader write-back) unless `no_save`;","locale":"en"},"5482":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-75","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#configuration","content":"offer the new config to the plugin's **update hook**;","locale":"en"},"5483":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-76","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#configuration","content":"if no hook accepts it, `restart()` (`revision += 1` → new target → unload → reload).","locale":"en"},"5484":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-77","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#configuration","content":"Hook registration is an effect: `ctx.on_config_update::<P>(|new| async { …; Ok(Handled::Yes) })`.","locale":"en"},"5485":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-78","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#configuration","content":"In the code, `Fiber::update(value)` and `Fiber::update_with(value, no_save)` return `Result<Settle>`: validation errors are returned at once, and the `Settle` future resolves when the update has taken effect (`db.update(json!(..))?.await?`). The update hook is `ctx.on_update::<C>(|new: &C| -> bool)` (returning `true` takes the update over, no restart) or `ctx.on_update_with::<C>(..)` (returning the future that `update` resolves with); `C` is the plugin's decoded configuration type. Before a plugin's own decoding, the configuration passes through the root's config hooks (`ctx.on_config(..)`), which the loader uses to evaluate expressions.","locale":"en"},"5486":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-79","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#lifecycle","content":"A faithful port of `_checkImpl` / `_refresh` / `_setEpoch` / `_reload` / `_unload` / `_updateState`, split into pure critical sections returning actions:","locale":"en"},"5487":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-80","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#lifecycle","content":"`begin` sets the state, creates the `inertia` shared future, and, on leaving `Active`, emits notifications for every provision still bound (L-Leave before any inverse).","locale":"en"},"5488":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-81","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#lifecycle","content":"`finish_reload`: if the target is unchanged → `Active` (or `Failed` if the apply errored; the error is recorded only if still current, so a superseded failure only cleans up); otherwise chain into `unload`.","locale":"en"},"5489":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-82","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#lifecycle","content":"`finish_unload`: drop self from each committed impl's `dependents` and wake providers whose set became empty; then `Inactive` (→ `Pending`/`Failed`/`Disposed` by `settled_state()`) or chain into `reload` if the target is no longer ⊥.","locale":"en"},"5490":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-83","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#lifecycle","content":"`wait_until` uses `Notify` with `Notified::enable()` registered *before* releasing the lock (no lost wake-ups).","locale":"en"},"5491":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-84","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#lifecycle","content":"The code splits the work slightly differently (`lifecycle.rs`). `refresh` computes the target and calls `set_target`, the port of `_setEpoch`, which records it and, unless the fiber has failed or a transition is in flight (`inertia: bool`), calls `begin_reload` (commit ω := resolved, register as dependent, record the episode, queue `Action::Reload`) or `begin_unload` (queue `Action::Unload`) and updates the state; `update_state` emits `StatusChanged` and, on entering or leaving `Active`, notifies the consumers of the fiber's live provisions. The spawned `reload` first yields (to coalesce superseded updates), then reads the plugin and its configuration (`prepare_execute`), resolves the configuration through the config hooks, runs `apply` with `catch_unwind`, and calls `finish_reload`. `unload` waits until no binding of the fiber has dependents, drains the accumulator newest first (`start_drain`), disposes all effects concurrently with `join_all`, logs failures, and calls `finish_unload`. `wait_until` registers `Notified::enable()` on the root-wide notifier before checking the predicate.","locale":"en"},"5492":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-85","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#instantiation","content":"`ctx.plugin(..)` creates `FiberData` (fresh `FiberId`, child `Scope`), resolves injections, and registers on the **parent** fiber an effect whose body inserts the fiber into its runtime and runs `refresh`, and whose inverse marks it removed, forces the target to ⊥, awaits the transition chain and settles to `Disposed` (TS `fiber.ts:178-206`). Unloading a parent therefore cascades to children through the parent's accumulator; re-enabling creates a *fresh* fiber (paper §4.4, Configuration).","locale":"en"},"5493":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-86","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#replace","content":"`registry.replace(old, new)` (aliases + `_replace` on each fiber: new runtime, new inject, re-resolve, clear error, `revision += 1`, refresh) is ported as is; it is the primitive behind HMR and is useful on its own (swapping an implementation behind live fibers).","locale":"en"},"5494":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-87","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#replace","content":"In the code: `ctx.registry().replace(&old_def, &new_def) -> Vec<Fiber>` returns the fibers moved to the new definition, and each moved fiber emits `Replaced`.","locale":"en"},"5495":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-88","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#fiber-handle","content":"In the code (`fiber.rs`): `context()` returns `&Context`. `settled()`, `restart()` and `fail(error)` return a `Settle` (a boxed future of `kardia_core::Result<()>`), `dispose()` a `Disposal` (a future of `Result<()>`; disposing the root fiber restarts it, reverting everything registered on it), and `update(config)` / `update_with(config, no_save)` a `Result<Settle>`. There are also `config()`, `inject()`, `error()`, `runtime()`, `def()`, `name()`, `parent()`, `is_removed()`, `is_root()`, `effect_handle()` and `effect_count()`. `ctx.fiber()` returns the fiber of a context.","locale":"en"},"5496":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-89","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#typed-events","content":"Listeners are stored in an `IdTable` per event `TypeId`; registration is an effect of the listener's fiber.","locale":"en"},"5497":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-90","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#typed-events","content":"Dispatch snapshots the table and skips removed listeners.","locale":"en"},"5498":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-91","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#typed-events","content":"Optional realm filtering (`E: Scoped`) reproduces `Context.filter` for `ServiceChanged`.","locale":"en"},"5499":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-92","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#typed-events","content":"Waterfall-style extension points (`internal/config`, `internal/get`, `internal/update`) are replaced by **explicit hook traits** (config transform hook for the loader, update hook per fiber, commit hook) rather than stringly-typed events.","locale":"en"},"5500":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-93","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#typed-events","content":"Internal events: `PluginInserted/Removed`, `StatusChanged { fiber, old, new }`, `ServiceChanged { key, realm }`, `ConfigCommitted { fiber, value }`, `Replaced { fiber }`.","locale":"en"},"5501":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-94","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#typed-events","content":"In the code (`events.rs`): `trait Event: Any + Send + Sync { type Output: Bailable; fn filter(&self, listener: &Context) -> bool { true } }`. `Bailable` (implemented for `()`, `bool`, `Option<T>` and `Result<T, Error>`) says which results stop a `bail` / `serial` dispatch and which count as failures of a `parallel` one. `filter` replaces `E: Scoped`: `ServiceChanged` reaches only listeners whose context resolves the key to the same realm. Listeners are kept in a `Vec` per `TypeId`. `on` returns `Result<EffectHandle>`; there are also `on_prepend`, `on_async`, `once` and `listener_count`. The hooks are `ctx.on_config(..)` (config transform), `ctx.on_update::<C>(..)` (update hook per fiber), and the `ConfigCommitted` event as the commit point. `ConfigCommitted` carries `{ fiber, config }`. `serial` returns `Result<Option<E::Output>>`: a panicking listener stops it with the panic as the error, while `emit` and `bail` log a panicking listener, synchronous or asynchronous, and continue (see Events).","locale":"en"},"5502":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-95","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#logger","content":"`Logger` is a core service:","locale":"en"},"5503":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-96","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#logger","content":"`ctx.logger(name)` → `Logger { name, level, fiber }` (name defaults to the calling fiber's runtime name, overridable by interception metadata `{ name, level }`);","locale":"en"},"5504":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-97","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#logger","content":"`Exporter` trait objects registered via `logger.exporter(e)` **as effects**;","locale":"en"},"5505":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-98","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#logger","content":"ring buffer of the last 1000 `Message { sn, ts, name, level, args }`.","locale":"en"},"5506":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-99","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#logger","content":"Format strings use Rust formatting (`format_args!`) instead of printf directives. A `tracing` bridge (a `tracing_subscriber::Layer` feeding exporters) can be added in `kardia-logger-console`; it is not required by core.","locale":"en"},"5507":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-100","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#logger","content":"In the code (`logger.rs`): `ctx.logger()` takes no name (it is named by `intercept(\"logger\", { name })` or after the fiber's plugin), `ctx.logger_named(name)` names it explicitly, and `svc.logger()` logs on a caller's behalf. Levels are `error`, `warn`, `info` and `debug`, and each takes `impl Display`, so `format_args!(..)` works directly. Exporters are registered with `ctx.add_exporter(Arc<dyn Exporter>)`, an effect of the current fiber; `Exporter::export(&Message)` and an optional per-name `level`. `Message` is `{ sn, ts, name, level, text, fiber }`: the text is already formatted (see Logger). The buffer holds 1000 messages by default (`ctx.log_buffer()`, `ctx.set_log_buffer_size(n)`). No `tracing` bridge has been added.","locale":"en"},"5508":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-101","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#built-ins","content":"Crate","locale":"en"},"5509":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-102","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#built-ins","content":"Port of","locale":"en"},"5510":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-103","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#built-ins","content":"Rust building blocks","locale":"en"},"5511":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-104","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#built-ins","content":"`kardia-timer`","locale":"en"},"5512":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-105","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#built-ins","content":"`plugin-timer`","locale":"en"},"5513":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-106","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#built-ins","content":"`tokio::time::{sleep, interval}`, `JoinHandle::abort` as disposers; `timeout(d) -> Result<(), Disposed>` future; `interval(d) -> impl Stream`; `throttle/debounce` handles","locale":"en"},"5514":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-107","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#built-ins","content":"`kardia-logger-console`","locale":"en"},"5515":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-108","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#built-ins","content":"`plugin-logger-console`","locale":"en"},"5516":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-109","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#built-ins","content":"ANSI colours, label alignment, level map","locale":"en"},"5517":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-110","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#built-ins","content":"`kardia-fs`","locale":"en"},"5518":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-111","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#built-ins","content":"`plugin-fs-node`","locale":"en"},"5519":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-112","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#built-ins","content":"`notify` (watch parent dir for single files), `globset` + ported last-match-wins/ancestor predicate","locale":"en"},"5520":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-113","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#built-ins","content":"The timer API in the code (`TimerExt` on `Svc<Timer>`): `timeout(delay, callback)` and `interval(delay, callback)` are effects returning `Result<EffectHandle>`; `sleep(delay)` is the future that fails with `Disposed` if the caller is disposed first; `ticks(delay)` returns `Ticks`, whose `tick().await` resolves on the next tick (instead of a `Stream`); `throttle` and `debounce` return `Scheduled<A>` handles. `ctx.timer()` (`TimerContext`) is shorthand for `ctx.get::<Timer>()`.","locale":"en"},"5521":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","type":"page","content":"Core data structures","breadcrumbs":["kardia Documentation","Development"],"tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","locale":"en"},"5522":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle-0","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","content":"Identifiers, scope overlays, bindings, fibers, targets and committed views, IdTable and dependency-cycle detection in kardia-core.","locale":"en"},"5523":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle-1","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle#identifiers","content":"Identifiers (`key.rs`)","locale":"en"},"5524":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle-2","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle#scope","content":"Scope overlays (`scope.rs`): derived realization and live re-parenting","locale":"en"},"5525":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle-3","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle#bindings","content":"Bindings (`store.rs`)","locale":"en"},"5526":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle-4","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle#fibers","content":"Fibers (`fiber.rs`)","locale":"en"},"5527":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle-5","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle#id-table","content":"`IdTable<T>`: commutative-by-construction registry","locale":"en"},"5528":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle-6","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle#cycle-detection","content":"Dependency-cycle detection (D9)","locale":"en"},"5529":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle-7","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","content":"The core data structures of `kardia-core`: the state the lifecycle runs on. Fibers live in a registry arena addressed by never-reused ids, and every cross reference (parent, dependents, committed view) is an id, never a pointer (decision **D2** of the guiding decisions). The algorithms that move a fiber through its states (refresh, reload, unload, instantiation) are on the API design page under The lifecycle state machine.","locale":"en"},"5530":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle-8","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","content":"The sketches on this page are the plan's design. The code keeps their meaning but not always their shape; a note under each sketch gives the current names and fields in `crates/kardia-core/src`.","locale":"en"},"5531":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle-9","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle#identifiers","content":"Never-reused ids implement the paper's atomic generative names and make \"replaced by an equal value\" observable (Def. 53).","locale":"en"},"5532":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle-10","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle#identifiers","content":"In the code: `Key`, `RealmId`, `ImplId` and `FiberId` exist as sketched (`FiberId::ROOT` is `0`, `RealmId::new()` creates a fresh realm). `RealmId` prints as `realm#n`; the readable labels `svc#id` / `svc@label` belong to the loader's realms. There is no `EffectId`: effects are `EffectCell`s held in an `IdTable`, whose ids are plain `u64`.","locale":"en"},"5533":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle-11","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle#scope","content":"This replaces JS prototype chains (`Object.create`, `setPrototypeOf`, `swap`). Own vs. inherited is explicit.","locale":"en"},"5534":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle-12","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle#scope","content":"In the code, a `Scope` is one node of a context's derivation chain: `Scope { parent: RwLock<Option<Arc<Scope>>>, kind: ScopeKind }`, with `ScopeKind::{Fiber(FiberId), Isolate(Key, RealmId), Intercept(Key, Value), Overlay(Arc<Overlay>)}`. Every derived context (a fiber's own context, `isolate`, `intercept`, `extend`) adds a node; re-pointing a parent moves everything below it. The loader's mutable per-entry tables are public `Overlay` nodes (`set_isolations`, `set_intercepts`), and the entry marker is an `Overlay::tagged(tag)` found again with `Context::tag()`. Because scope nodes carry their fiber, the fiber chain of an access is read off the derivation chain (see Re-pointable contexts).","locale":"en"},"5535":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle-13","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle#bindings","content":"The `type_id` check refers to paper §6.6.","locale":"en"},"5536":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle-14","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle#bindings","content":"In the code, both structs live in `state.rs`:","locale":"en"},"5537":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle-15","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle#bindings","content":"`Impl` has `key`, `realm`, `provider`, `provider_scope`, `value`, `check: Option<Check>`, `view: Option<View>` (a JSON reading of the value, for expressions and tooling) and `dependents`. It has no `id` (bindings are keyed by `ImplId` in `State::impls`), no `type_id` (a collision surfaces as `AccessError::TypeMismatch` when the value fails to downcast), and no `released` notifier (the unload guard waits on the root-wide notifier). `Check` is `Arc<dyn Fn(&[Value]) -> bool + Send + Sync>`: it receives the consumer's interception metadata for the key and runs under the lock, so it must not call into the runtime.","locale":"en"},"5538":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle-16","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle#bindings","content":"`State` has `fibers`, `runtimes: IndexMap<RuntimeKey, RuntimeData>` (registration order), `aliases: HashMap<RuntimeKey, PluginDef>`, `impls: HashMap<ImplId, Impl>` (every binding that still exists, live or withdrawn but still referenced), `store: HashMap<RealmId, ImplId>` (the live binding of each realm, the paper's Σ), `root_realms`, `listeners` (per event `TypeId`), `config_hooks`, id counters and the queued `actions`. There is no `props` table: accessors and mixins are not ported (see Associated properties).","locale":"en"},"5539":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle-17","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle#fibers","content":"`Fiber::set_recovery` now selects `Manual` (default), bounded `Retry`, or `Host` handling. The supervisor outlives the failed instance, waits for cleanup and cancels on disposal or policy changes. Loader entries expose the same choice through `recovery`; see configuration and examples and native fault evidence. `restart()` still does not clear failure; manual handling uses `update`, while selected supervision drives the supported recovery operation. Unrelated plugins must remain operational under every policy.","locale":"en"},"5540":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle-18","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle#fibers","content":"JS also implements `Fiber.setRecovery` and `Fiber.fail`, with supervision outside instance effects. Failure decisions follow cleanup and are cancelled on removal or policy changes. JS timers distinguish callback errors from instance failures and await in-flight callbacks during disposal. See the JS guide and cross-runtime acceptance.","locale":"en"},"5541":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle-19","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle#fibers","content":"In the code (`state.rs` and `fiber.rs`), `FiberState` and `Target` are as sketched, except that `Target::Epoch::providers` is a `Vec<ImplId>` (the workspace does not use `smallvec`). `FiberData` differs:","locale":"en"},"5542":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle-20","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle#fibers","content":"No `id` (it is the key of `State::fibers`), `runtime: Option<RuntimeKey>` (the root fiber has none), `inject: Inject`.","locale":"en"},"5543":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle-21","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle#fibers","content":"`config: Value` is the source configuration as given; it is decoded once per load attempt, not stored typed.","locale":"en"},"5544":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle-22","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle#fibers","content":"`episode: Option<Target>`: the target the current episode was loaded for, which the iteration checkpoints compare with.","locale":"en"},"5545":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle-23","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle#fibers","content":"`committed: Option<HashMap<Key, ImplId>>`; there is no `CommittedView` type.","locale":"en"},"5546":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle-24","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle#fibers","content":"`inertia: bool`: a transition is in flight. Waiting (`Fiber::settled()`, the unload guard) uses `RootInner::wait_until` on the root-wide notifier instead of a per-fiber `unload_gate` or `watch` channel.","locale":"en"},"5547":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle-25","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle#fibers","content":"`error: Option<Error>`.","locale":"en"},"5548":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle-26","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle#fibers","content":"`provisions: Vec<ImplId>`, plus `draining` and `withdrawn` (provisions withdrawn during a drain stay visible to the fiber's descendants until the drain is over).","locale":"en"},"5549":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle-27","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle#fibers","content":"`disposables: IdTable<Arc<EffectCell>>`, `update_hooks: IdTable<Arc<UpdateHook>>`, and `retire: Option<Arc<EffectCell>>` (the effect on the parent that instantiated the fiber, O-Insert).","locale":"en"},"5550":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle-28","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle#id-table","content":"The `DisposableList` design (insert returns a fresh id; remove by id; `drain_rev()` returns LIFO order) is exported publicly as `kardia_core::IdTable<T>`. It is what paper §3.4.2 calls a commutative key: service authors should build their registries (routes, listeners, commands) on it.","locale":"en"},"5551":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle-29","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle#cycle-detection","content":"On insertion, compute whether the precedence relation ≺ (`p_n ∩ d_m ≠ ∅`, from declared `provide`/`inject`) closes a cycle among non-disposed fibers in the same realm; if so, log a warning naming the cycle and leave the fiber `Pending` (behaviour otherwise unchanged). It only reads declarations, so it can be computed eagerly (paper §6.5). Declared `provide` is metadata only (as in Cordis); a plugin that provides undeclared keys simply escapes the check.","locale":"en"},"5552":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle-30","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle#cycle-detection","content":"Implemented in `registry.rs` (`warn_cycle`): a depth-first search along \"depends on a declared provider\" edges, logging `dependency cycle: a -> b -> …` at warning level.","locale":"en"},"5553":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","type":"page","content":"Loader, include and dynamic modules","breadcrumbs":["kardia Documentation","Development"],"tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-loader","locale":"en"},"5554":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-0","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader","content":"Internals of kardia-loader, kardia-include and kardia-hmr: the Rust adaptations of the Cordis loader, the file-backed include and the hot-reload algorithm with its module backends.","locale":"en"},"5555":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-1","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"`kardia-loader`","locale":"en"},"5556":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-2","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-loader#include","content":"`kardia-include`","locale":"en"},"5557":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-3","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-loader#hmr","content":"Dynamic modules and HMR (`kardia-hmr`)","locale":"en"},"5558":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-4","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader","content":"How the Cordis loader, include and HMR packages are ported: the data model, the entry state machine, file sync and the module graph. For how to use them, see the guide pages Loader, Hot reload and Guest plugins; for the TypeScript originals, see Loader, Include and group and HMR in the Cordis reference.","locale":"en"},"5559":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-5","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"Port of `packages/loader` with these Rust adaptations:","locale":"en"},"5560":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-6","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"Topic","locale":"en"},"5561":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-7","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"Adaptation","locale":"en"},"5562":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-8","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"**Data model**","locale":"en"},"5563":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-9","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"`EntryOptions` as a serde struct with `#[serde(flatten)] extra: IndexMap<String, Value>` (tolerate unknown keys); `Option<Option<T>>`/`Value::Null` for \"delete on merge\"; `IsolateLabel::{Private, Shared(String)}`; `Disabled::{Bool, Expr}`.","locale":"en"},"5564":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-10","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"**Id-addressed documents instead of object identity**","locale":"en"},"5565":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-11","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"TS relies on `entry.options === group.data[i]`; Rust keeps the group's `Vec<EntryOptions>` authoritative and entries hold ids; \"came from the file\" is a provenance flag, not `===`.","locale":"en"},"5566":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-12","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"**Entry state machine**","locale":"en"},"5567":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-13","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"`update / _patchContext / _reconcile / init / _init / _start` ported 1:1 with the \"re-check after every await\" style (`fn still_current(&self, snap) -> bool`), `_initTask` as `Option<Shared<..>>`.","locale":"en"},"5568":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-14","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"**Two fibers per entry with `inject`**","locale":"en"},"5569":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-15","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"The outer dependency holder + the inner plugin are kept: the outer `apply` resolves the plugin from the catalog, hits `ctx.checkpoint().await?`, then starts the inner fiber.","locale":"en"},"5570":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-16","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"**Catalog**","locale":"en"},"5571":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-17","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"Replaces module import: `Catalog::register::<P>()` / `register_alias(\"@cordisjs/plugin-group\", Group)`; a missing name yields `ModuleNotFound` located at the entry's `name`.","locale":"en"},"5572":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-18","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"**Managed realms**","locale":"en"},"5573":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-19","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"`LocalRealm`/`GlobalRealm` as `RealmId`s with labels; the 7-step `patch-context` algorithm ported faithfully, with the delimiter test computed directly by walking scope parents (`scope.is_within(entry_marker)`) instead of temporary symbols; provider migration without restart; blocked moves tracked in `Impl::realm`. **Fix** the realm-GC early `return` (use `continue`).","locale":"en"},"5574":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-20","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"**Self-dispose / self-update write-back**","locale":"en"},"5575":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-21","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"Core passes a `DisposeReason { SelfRequested, Parent, Loader }` instead of the TS `_disposing` flag + unregister-before-dispose trick (a deliberate breaking simplification).","locale":"en"},"5576":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-22","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"**`loader` await barrier**","locale":"en"},"5577":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-23","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"`Loader::settled().await` and an availability check driven by an atomic task counter.","locale":"en"},"5578":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-24","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"**Validation**","locale":"en"},"5579":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-25","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"`validate_options(&Value, deep) -> Vec<Issue>` at the boundary (deep for API calls, shallow for file input).","locale":"en"},"5580":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-26","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"**Expressions** (`!!js`)","locale":"en"},"5581":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-27","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"JS `with(ctx) eval` cannot be ported. Decision point (Risks): adopt a side-effect-free expression language (recommended: CEL, or a small custom evaluator) whose identifiers resolve through `ctx.get_dyn(name)` and a serializable service view (`trait ExprValue { fn to_value(&self) -> Value }`), respecting the same evaluation-context table as the TS loader. Until then, `Expr` values are rejected with a clear validation issue.","locale":"en"},"5582":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-28","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"Where `kardia-loader` differs from the table:","locale":"en"},"5583":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-29","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"**Data model:** `EntryOptions` is a typed view read from options already accepted by `validate_options` (`EntryOptions::read`); the JSON object stays the source of truth, so unknown keys survive write-back without a `flatten` map. `IsolateLabel::{Private, Shared(String)}` and `Disabled::{Bool(bool), Expr(String)}` are as planned.","locale":"en"},"5584":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-30","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"**Catalog:** `Catalog::register::<P>()`, `register_def(name, def)` and `alias(alias, name)` (not `register_alias`); `kardia_loader::builtins()` registers `group` and aliases `@cordisjs/plugin-group` to it, and `kardia::catalog()` adds the other built-in plugins under their names and their JavaScript package names.","locale":"en"},"5585":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-31","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"**Checkpoint:** synchronous, `ctx.checkpoint()?`.","locale":"en"},"5586":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-32","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"**Managed realms:** the delimiter test is `Context::is_within(..)` on contexts whose entry tables are tagged `Overlay` nodes; the realm-GC fix is in (`collect_realms` uses `continue`).","locale":"en"},"5587":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-33","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"**Self-dispose:** `DisposeReason` was not introduced. The loader sets a per-entry `disposing` flag around its own `fiber.dispose()` call (`entry.rs`, `dispose_fiber`), the same pattern as the TS `_disposing` flag.","locale":"en"},"5588":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-34","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"**Expressions:** implemented as a side-effect-free JavaScript subset (`kardia_loader::expr`) instead of CEL; identifiers resolve through the JSON views of services (`ctx.view(name)`, provided with `provide_serialized_as`), not through an `ExprValue` trait; see Expressions.","locale":"en"},"5589":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-35","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"**Key order:** options are `serde_json` maps, and `sort_keys` gives write-backs their stable order (`id`, `name`, the others alphabetically, `config` last) only with the `preserve-order` feature, which turns on `serde_json/preserve_order` and is on by default. Cargo unifies that feature into every `serde_json::Map` of a build, so it is optional: without it, maps and write-backs list their keys alphabetically. `kardia-include` and the `kardia` facade turn it on; `kardia-hmr` passes it through as a default feature, so an application that turns off `kardia-hmr`'s (and `kardia-loader`'s) default features keeps serde_json's default maps.","locale":"en"},"5590":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-36","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#include","content":"**Pure modules**, ported almost verbatim and unit-tested in isolation: `journal` (record, merge, flatten, apply, three-way `reconcile`; file wins), `patch` (insert/override patches, `PatchIndex` ownership, `route_journal`), anonymous id assignment.","locale":"en"},"5591":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-37","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#include","content":"**IO loop:** one task driven by `dirty_read/dirty_write` flags + `Notify`; latest-wins tree application; `flush()`.","locale":"en"},"5592":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-38","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#include","content":"**Parsing:** a YAML parser with spans and custom tags (`saphyr` family): `!!js` → `Value::Expr`; errors **and** warnings fatal; JSON via `serde_json` plus a spanned pass for locations.","locale":"en"},"5593":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-39","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#include","content":"**Atomic writes:** temp file in the same directory, stale-content check before every rename, bounded retry on `PermissionDenied`/`ResourceBusy`, read-only → keep journal in memory.","locale":"en"},"5594":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-40","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#include","content":"**Errors:** structured `ConfigFrame { id, file, line, col }` chains instead of rewritten stack strings.","locale":"en"},"5595":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-41","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#include","content":"In the code, `serde_json::Value` has no expression variant: `!!js` becomes the object `{ \"__jsExpr\": \"…\" }` (`kardia_loader::EXPR_KEY`). Errors are `ConfigFileError` / `ConfigIssue` values whose locations are `file:line:col`, and the loader prints located frames as text (`at <id> (file:line:col)`) rather than a `ConfigFrame` type; see Errors.","locale":"en"},"5596":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-42","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#hmr","content":"Optional, Phase 7. Paper §6.4: *native code exposes no module registry, so introduction and retraction take the form of explicit dynamic linking (dlopen/dlclose) … WebAssembly … released when a native embedder (e.g. Wasmtime) drops it.* The plan separates the **algorithm** (portable) from the **module technology** (a backend):","locale":"en"},"5597":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-43","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#hmr","content":"In the code (`graph.rs`): `trait ModuleSource: Send + Sync + 'static { fn load(&self, id: &str) -> Result<ModuleCode>; }`. `ModuleId` is a `String` (an absolute path), `ModuleCode { deps: Vec<ModuleId>, evaluate }` evaluates against its dependencies' exports, and a module exports `Exports::{None, Plugin(PluginDef), Value(Value)}`. `ModuleGraph::new(source)` keeps an ordered table of loaded records (`deps`, `exports`) and the set of host modules.","locale":"en"},"5598":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-44","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#hmr","content":"The host owns a `ModuleGraph { records: HashMap<ModuleId, ModuleRecord { deps, kind: Host|Dynamic, instance } > }` replacing Node's caches.","locale":"en"},"5599":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-45","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#hmr","content":"The Cordis reconcile algorithm is ported as pure graph code with tests from `reconcile.spec.ts`. This follows the **code**, not the paper's Algorithm 10 (see HMR: paper vs. code).","locale":"en"},"5600":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-46","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#hmr","content":"Transactions are serialized, and re-entrancy is rejected via a task-local transaction id.","locale":"en"},"5601":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-47","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#hmr","content":"Backends, in order of preference:","locale":"en"},"5602":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-48","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#hmr","content":"**WASM components (wasmtime):** old and new instances coexist safely, dropping is sound, natural sandbox (paper §6.3 bridge fibers). Cost: services cross a WIT boundary.","locale":"en"},"5603":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-49","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#hmr","content":"**dylibs (`libloading`):** development-time only: same toolchain, each version loaded from a unique copied path, **never unloaded** (unloading Rust dylibs is unsound with TLS destructors and leaked `'static` references), C-ABI entry point.","locale":"en"},"5604":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-50","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#hmr","content":"**Out-of-process plugins** (respawn = reload) via the service-broker/RPC pattern of paper §6.2.","locale":"en"},"5605":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-51","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#hmr","content":"Statically linked plugins are \"host modules\" by construction; changing them requires a process restart, while live *re*configuration remains fully supported by the loader.","locale":"en"},"5606":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-52","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#hmr","content":"All three backends exist: `WasmSource` (core WASM modules, not WIT components), `DylibSource` and `ProcessSource`, sharing one JSON guest ABI, plus `kardia-guest` for writing guests in Rust. Watch mode is `HmrConfig { base, watch }`. The full account of how they differ from this plan is under Dynamic modules.","locale":"en"},"5607":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","type":"page","content":"Risks and worked example","breadcrumbs":["kardia Documentation","Development"],"tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-risks","locale":"en"},"5608":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-0","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-risks","content":"The risks and open decisions of the kardia design with their proposed answers, and a worked example of the plugin API.","locale":"en"},"5609":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-1","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-risks#risks","content":"Risks and open decisions","locale":"en"},"5610":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-2","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-risks#worked-example","content":"Worked example","locale":"en"},"5611":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-3","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-risks#risks","content":"Topic","locale":"en"},"5612":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-4","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-risks#risks","content":"Risk / question","locale":"en"},"5613":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-5","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-risks#risks","content":"Proposed answer","locale":"en"},"5614":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-6","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-risks#risks","content":"Threading model","locale":"en"},"5615":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-7","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-risks#risks","content":"`Send` bounds everywhere vs. a literal JS-like `!Send` design","locale":"en"},"5616":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-8","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-risks#risks","content":"`Send + Sync` with the lock-plus-actions rule (Concurrency); revisit only with evidence","locale":"en"},"5617":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-9","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-risks#risks","content":"Reentrancy","locale":"en"},"5618":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-10","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-risks#risks","content":"user code called under the state lock deadlocks (`parking_lot` is not reentrant)","locale":"en"},"5619":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-11","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-risks#risks","content":"enforced by structure: critical sections return `Actions`; availability `check` closures documented as runtime-free","locale":"en"},"5620":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-12","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-risks#risks","content":"Disposal order","locale":"en"},"5621":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-13","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-risks#risks","content":"paper LIFO vs. Cordis concurrent fiber-level disposal","locale":"en"},"5622":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-14","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-risks#risks","content":"follow Cordis (tests depend on it); LIFO within one effect","locale":"en"},"5623":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-15","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-risks#risks","content":"Cancellation","locale":"en"},"5624":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-16","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-risks#risks","content":"dropping `apply` futures would leak half-registered resources","locale":"en"},"5625":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-17","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-risks#risks","content":"inertial host + explicit checkpoints (Interruption)","locale":"en"},"5626":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-18","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-risks#risks","content":"Reference cycles","locale":"en"},"5627":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-19","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-risks#risks","content":"services hold their own `Context` (→ `Root`), `Root` holds services","locale":"en"},"5628":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-20","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-risks#risks","content":"cycles are broken when bindings are withdrawn; `Root::shutdown().await` disposes the root fiber and clears the store","locale":"en"},"5629":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-21","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-risks#risks","content":"Expressions","locale":"en"},"5630":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-22","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-risks#risks","content":"no JS `eval`","locale":"en"},"5631":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-23","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-risks#risks","content":"decide on CEL vs. custom evaluator before Phase 4 finishes; reject expressions until then","locale":"en"},"5632":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-24","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-risks#risks","content":"Dynamic code","locale":"en"},"5633":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-25","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-risks#risks","content":"Rust has no module registry; dylib unloading unsound","locale":"en"},"5634":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-26","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-risks#risks","content":"static catalog first; WASM backend for real hot reload/sandboxing","locale":"en"},"5635":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-27","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-risks#risks","content":"Type-level keys","locale":"en"},"5636":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-28","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-risks#risks","content":"string keys can collide; `TypeId` unstable across dylibs","locale":"en"},"5637":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-29","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-risks#risks","content":"`TypeId` check in-process; string + version tag for dynamic modules (paper §6.6)","locale":"en"},"5638":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-30","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-risks#risks","content":"Error-message parity","locale":"en"},"5639":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-31","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-risks#risks","content":"TS messages/columns (UTF-16)","locale":"en"},"5640":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-32","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-risks#risks","content":"keep message wording where cheap; no UTF-16 parity","locale":"en"},"5641":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-33","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-risks#risks","content":"How these were settled in the code:","locale":"en"},"5642":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-34","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-risks#risks","content":"**Reentrancy:** critical sections queue `Action`s on the state (see Concurrency); values released under the lock are dropped after it.","locale":"en"},"5643":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-35","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-risks#risks","content":"**Reference cycles:** there is no `Root` type or `shutdown()`. Disposing the root fiber, `root.fiber().dispose().await`, restarts it and reverts everything registered on it, which withdraws every binding.","locale":"en"},"5644":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-36","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-risks#risks","content":"**Expressions:** decided as a side-effect-free JavaScript subset (`kardia_loader::expr`), not CEL; see Status and deviations.","locale":"en"},"5645":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-37","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-risks#risks","content":"**Dynamic code:** the static catalog came first; the WASM, dylib and process backends followed (see Dynamic modules).","locale":"en"},"5646":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-38","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-risks#risks","content":"**Type-level keys:** the in-process check is the downcast at access (`AccessError::TypeMismatch`). Guest services cross the module boundary as JSON values with JSON calls (`GuestService`), so no Rust type or `TypeId` crosses it.","locale":"en"},"5647":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-39","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-risks#worked-example","content":"A provider and a consumer, written against the plugin API. The first tab is the example in today's API (it compiles and runs against the workspace once `connect` and its pool, placeholders for a real database client, are supplied); the second is the plan's original sketch of the then-future API.","locale":"en"},"5648":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-40","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-risks#worked-example","content":"What changed between the sketch and the code:","locale":"en"},"5649":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-41","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-risks#worked-example","content":"**Root:** `Context::new()` creates the root context; `root.fiber().dispose()` replaces `root.shutdown()`.","locale":"en"},"5650":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-42","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-risks#worked-example","content":"**Errors:** `Result<()>` (`kardia_core::Result`) replaces `Result<(), PluginError>` and `anyhow::Result`.","locale":"en"},"5651":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-43","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-risks#worked-example","content":"**Declarations:** `fn inject() -> Inject` (here `Inject::of::<(Database,)>()`) replaces `type Inject`, and `fn provide() -> Vec<Key>` replaces the `&'static [&'static str]` slice. `#[derive(Service)]` implements `Service` (the hand-written `impl Service for Database` also works). With the `#[plugin]` macro, a plugin is an `async fn` taking `Injected<(Database,)>`, which turns undeclared access into a compile error (see Plugins).","locale":"en"},"5652":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-44","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-risks#worked-example","content":"**Boundaries:** `ctx.checkpoint()?` and `ctx.on_dispose(..)?` are synchronous and fallible.","locale":"en"},"5653":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-45","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-risks#worked-example","content":"**Configuration:** `ctx.plugin::<P>(config)` requires `P::Config: Serialize` (the value is serialized and decoded like any other configuration), so a `Deserialize`-only config is passed as JSON with `plugin_value`. `db.update(value)?` validates at once and returns the future to await; `update_with(value, no_save)` takes the flag.","locale":"en"},"5654":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-46","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-risks#worked-example","content":"**Settling:** `greeter.settled()` resolves as soon as no transition is in flight, so the example first waits for `db` to settle; the greeter's transitions start once the database is provided.","locale":"en"},"5655":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-47","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-risks#worked-example","content":"**Logger:** `ctx.logger_named(\"greeter\")`, or `ctx.logger()`, which is named after the plugin.","locale":"en"},"5656":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-48","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-risks#worked-example","content":"**Dependencies:** `#[derive(Deserialize)]` needs `serde` with the `derive` feature among the crate's own dependencies, even though `kardia::prelude` re-exports the derive.","locale":"en"},"5657":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","type":"page","content":"Server architecture and acceptance","breadcrumbs":["kardia Documentation","Development"],"tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-server","locale":"en"},"5658":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-0","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-server","content":"Lifecycle boundaries, fixed upstream comparisons, native platform evidence and documented differences.","locale":"en"},"5659":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-1","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-server#architecture","content":"Dependencies and ownership","locale":"en"},"5660":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-2","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-server#isolation","content":"Isolation and recovery","locale":"en"},"5661":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-3","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-server#parity","content":"Fixed comparisons and reproduction","locale":"en"},"5662":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-4","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-server#migration","content":"Synchronization and migration","locale":"en"},"5663":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-5","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-server#evidence","content":"Acceptance evidence","locale":"en"},"5664":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-6","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-server#earlier-acceptance-notes","content":"Earlier acceptance notes","locale":"en"},"5665":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-7","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-server#boundaries","content":"Relationship to upstream","locale":"en"},"5666":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-8","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-server","content":"The current fixed reference is Cordis `36dc7fb5e271143039e6b8b6df59840fc0886c1b`. The November 15 synchronization updates path decoding, Proxy confinement and FS directory semantics across Static, include, HMR and CLI module resolution. It also fixes repeated Rust effect disposal; the corresponding upstream fix is still on an unmerged branch. All five Server packages remain native Rust implementations. No version bump or release was performed.","locale":"en"},"5667":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-9","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-server#architecture","content":"`kardia-server` uses Hyper 1.12 for HTTP/1 and tokio-tungstenite 0.30 for WS frames. Kardia controls routing, claimed responses and lifecycle. `kardia-http` uses Reqwest 0.13.5, rustls platform certificate verification and explicit streaming decompression, preventing library redirect/status defaults from changing upstream semantics. Production code does not invoke Node.","locale":"en"},"5668":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-10","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-server#architecture","content":"The `Svc` caller owns route, mount, connection and stream effects; services resolve dependencies at their definition site. In-flight HTTP handlers, middleware, route/upgrade hooks and response sources belong to cancellable task collections. Disposal unregisters, stops and awaits destruction before recovery creates another instance. Streams use bounded channels; reader/poll panics become operation errors.","locale":"en"},"5669":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-11","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-server#architecture","content":"Static reads through FS providers. Content detection owns a separate stream. Simple file-type rules and extension mappings are generated from fixed sources, while Rust stream readers handle complex containers. A source-hash check prevents unaudited upstream changes from entering generated mappings. The default POSIX detail of picomatch-rs 0.1.0 is adjusted against real comparisons.","locale":"en"},"5670":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-12","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-server#isolation","content":"AGENTS.md makes isolation and configurable recovery mandatory. Manual is the default. Retry supervision lives outside the instance, waits for cleanup and uses a bounded rate. Host callbacks choose failure retention or delayed retry. Configuration errors do not loop. Policy changes, disablement, removal and disposal cancel old actions, and recovery does not rewrite configuration.","locale":"en"},"5671":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-13","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-server#isolation","content":"Area","locale":"en"},"5672":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-14","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-server#isolation","content":"Real verification","locale":"en"},"5673":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-15","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-server#isolation","content":"Core supervision / loader","locale":"en"},"5674":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-16","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-server#isolation","content":"All policies, repeated failure, invalid configuration, synchronous/asynchronous host panic, cancellation and unknown handlers","locale":"en"},"5675":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-17","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-server#isolation","content":"Server","locale":"en"},"5676":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-18","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-server#isolation","content":"Handler panic before/after claiming; occupied-port startup failure/recovery under every policy while an independent Server handles HTTP","locale":"en"},"5677":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-19","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-server#isolation","content":"HTTP lifecycle","locale":"en"},"5678":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-20","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-server#isolation","content":"Pending callbacks, body sources, middleware, route hooks and upgrade hooks are destroyed before disposal completes","locale":"en"},"5679":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-21","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-server#isolation","content":"Echo / ACL","locale":"en"},"5680":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-22","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-server#isolation","content":"Repeated instance failure under every policy with healthy routes; Echo preserves the same healthy WS connection","locale":"en"},"5681":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-23","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-server#isolation","content":"FS / Static","locale":"en"},"5682":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-24","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-server#isolation","content":"Provider invocation/poll panic, first/later read errors, cancellation and non-local mounts; stream counters return to zero","locale":"en"},"5683":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-25","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-server#isolation","content":"HTTP client / Proxy","locale":"en"},"5684":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-26","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-server#isolation","content":"Repeated provider/instance failure under every policy, consumer rebuilding, continued healthy HTTP and the same healthy WS connection","locale":"en"},"5685":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-27","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-server#isolation","content":"Unavailable upstream","locale":"en"},"5686":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-28","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-server#isolation","content":"Current HTTP operation fails, WS never accepts; Proxy stays Active and works again without restart","locale":"en"},"5687":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-29","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-server#isolation","content":"Existing guest boundaries","locale":"en"},"5688":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-30","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-server#isolation","content":"Workspace regressions cover process exit/timeout/I/O panic, WASM and dylib paths; native dylibs are not described as crash-isolated","locale":"en"},"5689":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-31","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-server#isolation","content":"Tests inject errors, panics or Fiber failure into the actual implementation and observe real sockets and cleanup counters. Automatic recovery does not use test-driven updates. Manual mode is observed Failed before explicit handling. Host mode follows callback decisions. Only the failed instance and consumers of unavailable services may be interrupted.","locale":"en"},"5690":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-32","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-server#isolation","content":"The containment boundary remains explicit: in-process unwind handling can catch Rust panic, but cannot contain abort, segfault or process-wide exit. Use the existing process/WASM guest boundary when that isolation is needed. This work does not add a sandbox for arbitrary native code.","locale":"en"},"5691":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-33","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-server#parity","content":"Upstream comparisons live in the standalone `.github/workflows/server-reference.yml` workflow for manual verification. Select **Server reference → Run workflow** in GitHub Actions to run the Linux, Windows and macOS matrix. Its only trigger is `workflow_dispatch`; pushes, pull requests and releases do not invoke it. Regular Rust checks and failure-isolation tests remain in the main CI workflow.","locale":"en"},"5692":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-34","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-server#parity","content":"Build the Rust host from the repository root, then run the existing tooling from `js/`:","locale":"en"},"5693":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-35","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-server#parity","content":"Preparation verifies/builds the fixed upstream in ignored `cordis/`, installs immutable Yarn dependencies and runs the original suites. It does not reset an unknown upstream worktree. `inventory.json` uses the pinned TypeScript parser to expand `it.each` into actual cases; the inventory is not passing-test evidence. Adapters register fixtures and drive real services; they do not reproduce the algorithms under test.","locale":"en"},"5694":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-36","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-server#parity","content":"Test category","locale":"en"},"5695":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-37","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-server#parity","content":"Passing coverage","locale":"en"},"5696":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-38","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-server#parity","content":"Original upstream suites","locale":"en"},"5697":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-39","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-server#parity","content":"Server 111: core 58, ACL 15, Static 25, Proxy 13; plus FS 34 (one Windows-only case skipped on Linux/macOS)","locale":"en"},"5698":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-40","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-server#parity","content":"Supplement / dual-host network tests","locale":"en"},"5699":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-41","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-server#parity","content":"224 on Linux/macOS, 226 on Windows: original 201 plus Proxy 10, Static 2 and FS 11 (two extra FS cases on Windows)","locale":"en"},"5700":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-42","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-server#parity","content":"Content MIME detection","locale":"en"},"5701":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-43","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-server#parity","content":"254 files at fixture SHA `7fc6d9ff7cc01987aa73006757792a5ca198023a` plus 2 supplements, covering all 171 MIME values","locale":"en"},"5702":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-44","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-server#parity","content":"Negotiation and globs","locale":"en"},"5703":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-45","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-server#parity","content":"All 1,180 extensions; 26 Static configurations across 15 paths each; no semantic-difference normalization","locale":"en"},"5704":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-46","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-server#parity","content":"Native platforms","locale":"en"},"5705":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-47","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-server#parity","content":"Linux, Windows and macOS run Rust checks and actual upstream/comparison suites","locale":"en"},"5706":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-48","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-server#parity","content":"CLI / catalog","locale":"en"},"5707":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-49","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-server#parity","content":"A real CLI subprocess runs the example; catalog aliases, provider replacement and consumer rebuilding","locale":"en"},"5708":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-50","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-server#parity","content":"HTTP comparisons include status, reason, semantic headers and complete bodies; large files use full-body SHA-256. WS compares text/binary types, bytes, subprotocols and close code/reason. HTTPS/WSS uses a real certificate fixture, with separate rejection tests for untrusted certificates and hostname mismatch. Certificate verification is not bypassed.","locale":"en"},"5709":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-51","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-server#migration","content":"Path parameters preserve `+`; query form decoding still maps it to space. Proxy encodes each path segment and rejects destinations outside its target directory before connecting.","locale":"en"},"5710":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-52","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-server#migration","content":"FS bases/intercepts and Static roots are directories. Include scopes children to each file's directory; CLI guests and HMR share it. Replace `hmr.base` with `intercept.fs.baseUrl`. Native watch still accepts native paths; HTTP client base URLs retain ordinary URL semantics.","locale":"en"},"5711":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-53","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-server#migration","content":"`Resolver::resolve` now receives `&Context`; tree `base_dir` APIs were removed. Rust effect disposal starts once, waits for pending setup/cleanup, shares errors and continues after a caller drops its handle.","locale":"en"},"5712":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-54","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-server#migration","content":"Manual/default, bounded retry and host-defined recovery remain available; all require failure isolation and continued progress of unrelated plugins. No JS production package was added.","locale":"en"},"5713":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-55","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-server#evidence","content":"Rust CI #43709794937 passed all three native platforms at `aa14386`, including the full workspace, failure/recovery tests, no-default-feature checks, clippy, formatting and architecture.","locale":"en"},"5714":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-56","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-server#evidence","content":"Current synchronization: manually dispatched Server reference #43709881683 passed on Linux, Windows and macOS at `aa14386`. The dual-host suite passed 224 cases on Linux/macOS and 226 on Windows; the Windows run also executed the upstream drive-path test. JS CI #43709794978 passed at the same commit.","locale":"en"},"5715":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-57","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-server#evidence","content":"Local related Rust suites, workspace/all-target clippy, architecture and formatting checks passed. Website type checks and 41 tests passed.","locale":"en"},"5716":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-58","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-server#earlier-acceptance-notes","content":"Historical baseline: all six platform jobs in CI #43705092966 passed at `3226312`; JS CI #43705092953 passed at the same commit.","locale":"en"},"5717":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-59","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-server#earlier-acceptance-notes","content":"Local `cargo test --workspace`, workspace clippy, fmt, arch, loader tests/clippy without default features, and HMR process-feature clippy passed.","locale":"en"},"5718":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-60","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-server#earlier-acceptance-notes","content":"Initial FS Windows acceptance found NotFound for a file used as a parent path, versus Unix NotADirectory. Native expectations were corrected and CI #43700024799 passed; error mapping was not hidden.","locale":"en"},"5719":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-61","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-server#earlier-acceptance-notes","content":"Static's first macOS run timed out in an existing include rename-watcher test. Rerunning only the failed job at the same commit passed CI #43700396222. The final commit passed all platforms again.","locale":"en"},"5720":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-62","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-server#earlier-acceptance-notes","content":"A locally cached xtask binary contained the repository's old path. Cleaning only that package's build cache restored the architecture test, and the complete workspace then passed without changing assertions.","locale":"en"},"5721":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-63","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-server#earlier-acceptance-notes","content":"Cancelled intermediate runs are not success evidence. Native CI runners are not the user's personal devices; this work did not independently test those devices.","locale":"en"},"5722":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-64","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-server#boundaries","content":"Observed upstream details are retained, including dropped custom reason text for returned Response, Proxy's last Set-Cookie, provider-context proxy publicUrl, and error-page path rules that differ from ordinary files. See the behavior reference.","locale":"en"},"5723":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-65","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-server#boundaries","content":"Rust uses typed callbacks/services and UTF-8/serde values rather than reconstructing the entire JS object model. The auxiliary HTTP service covers HTTP(S)/WS(S) raw transport, configuration and lifecycle, not the complete decoder/file/data/SOCKS ecosystem; its WS client does not offer permessage-deflate. FS adds read-only provider streams and keeps existing watches, without a write ecosystem. All five target plugins have production implementations; no parallel JS products or guest network protocol were added.","locale":"en"},"5724":{"id":"/en/docs/development-server-66","page_id":"/en/docs/development-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-server#boundaries","content":"When changing the upstream SHA, record behavior changes and update shared fixtures before regenerating mappings and running native parity and failure matrices. Keep rules, fixture provenance and reproducible commands available. Compilation or a successful host restart cannot replace isolation acceptance.","locale":"en"},"5725":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","type":"page","content":"Status and deviations","breadcrumbs":["kardia Documentation","Development"],"tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-status","locale":"en"},"5726":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-0","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status","content":"The implementation phases of kardia, their exit criteria, the implementation status, and every place where the implementation departs from the plan or from the TypeScript code.","locale":"en"},"5727":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-1","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-status#phases","content":"Implementation phases","locale":"en"},"5728":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-2","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-status#status","content":"Implementation status","locale":"en"},"5729":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-3","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"Deviations","locale":"en"},"5730":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-4","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-status#errors","content":"Errors","locale":"en"},"5731":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-5","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-status#loader-hooks","content":"Loader hooks","locale":"en"},"5732":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-6","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-status#dynamic-modules","content":"Dynamic modules","locale":"en"},"5733":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-7","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-status#typescript","content":"The TypeScript side","locale":"en"},"5734":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-8","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status","content":"The plan was carried out in phases, each closed by porting and passing the matching part of the TypeScript test suite. All phases are done; this page records them and every place where the result departs from the design or from Cordis.","locale":"en"},"5735":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-9","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status","content":"The historical phases below describe the original core port. The subsequent Server implementation has passed fixed upstream and three-platform acceptance: all five packages, auxiliary HTTP/FS paths, catalog/CLI integration, and configurable recovery. File URLs and Windows native path behavior now have real comparisons; Server middleware provides typed continuation chains.","locale":"en"},"5736":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-10","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#phases","content":"Phase","locale":"en"},"5737":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-11","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#phases","content":"Scope","locale":"en"},"5738":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-12","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#phases","content":"Exit criteria","locale":"en"},"5739":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-13","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#phases","content":"**0. Scaffold**","locale":"en"},"5740":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-14","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#phases","content":"Workspace, crates, CI (fmt, clippy, test)","locale":"en"},"5741":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-15","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#phases","content":"empty crates build","locale":"en"},"5742":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-16","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#phases","content":"**1. Effects & store**","locale":"en"},"5743":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-17","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#phases","content":"ids, `Root`/`Context`/`Scope`, `IdTable`, effect engine (`effect_sync`, `effect`, `EffectScope`, `EffectHandle`), `provide`/`try_get_live`, isolate/intercept overlays; root fiber only","locale":"en"},"5744":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-18","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#phases","content":"`dispose.spec` ported and green","locale":"en"},"5745":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-19","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#phases","content":"**2. Fibers & lifecycle**","locale":"en"},"5746":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-20","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#phases","content":"`Plugin` trait, registry/runtimes, `FiberData`, target/committed views, refresh/reload/unload with inertia, two-level guard, failure, `update`/`restart`, instantiation cascade, `replace`, checked `get` (Algorithm 6), cycle warning","locale":"en"},"5747":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-21","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#phases","content":"Phase-2 specs green; model-based property tests (Testing) green","locale":"en"},"5748":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-22","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#phases","content":"**3. Services & built-ins**","locale":"en"},"5749":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-23","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#phases","content":"`Service`/`Svc<S>` caller attribution, interception merge, typed events + internal events, logger + exporters, `kardia-timer`, `kardia-logger-console`","locale":"en"},"5750":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-24","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#phases","content":"Phase-3 specs green; example app with a provider/consumer pair and hot provider replacement","locale":"en"},"5751":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-25","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#phases","content":"**4. Loader**","locale":"en"},"5752":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-26","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#phases","content":"`EntryOptions`, validation, `EntryTree`/`EntryGroup`/`Group`, entry state machine, outer/inner fibers, catalog, managed realms + migration, write-back hooks, `loader` barrier; `kardia` binary bootstrapping a root with the loader","locale":"en"},"5753":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-27","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#phases","content":"loader specs (minus expressions) green","locale":"en"},"5754":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-28","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#phases","content":"**5. Include & fs**","locale":"en"},"5755":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-29","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#phases","content":"`kardia-fs` watcher; `kardia-include` parse/journal/patch/sync/atomic write/locations; `kardia.yml` bootstrap identical to `core/bin.js`","locale":"en"},"5756":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-30","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#phases","content":"include + fs specs green; editing `kardia.yml` live reconfigures a running app","locale":"en"},"5757":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-31","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#phases","content":"**6. Ergonomics**","locale":"en"},"5758":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-32","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#phases","content":"`kardia-macros`: `#[plugin]`/`#[service]` derive (NAME, inject tuples, extension traits), compile-time undeclared-access errors for typed deps; optional expression language","locale":"en"},"5759":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-33","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#phases","content":"macro tests (trybuild)","locale":"en"},"5760":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-34","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#phases","content":"**7. Dynamic modules (optional)**","locale":"en"},"5761":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-35","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#phases","content":"`ModuleSource`, `ModuleGraph`, ported HMR reconcile; WASM backend prototype; dylib dev backend","locale":"en"},"5762":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-36","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#phases","content":"`reconcile.spec` ported against an in-memory source","locale":"en"},"5763":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-37","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#phases","content":"Phases 1–3 deliver the paper's core library; 4–5 the paper's loader; 7 the paper's HMR.","locale":"en"},"5764":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-38","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#phases","content":"Names as they ended up: `try_get_live` is `live`, the root is created with `Context::new()` (there is no public `Root`), and the service derive is `#[derive(Service)]`. The example app of Phase 3 is `crates/kardia/examples/provider_consumer.rs` (`cargo run -p kardia --example provider_consumer`).","locale":"en"},"5765":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-39","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#status","content":"All phases above are implemented in `crates/` (`cargo test --workspace`; `cargo run -p kardia -- examples/kardia.yml` boots an app from a watched config file).","locale":"en"},"5766":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-40","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#status","content":"Every case of every TypeScript spec has been ported, found already covered, or judged not applicable. The not-applicable cases test JavaScript or Node mechanics with no Rust counterpart:","locale":"en"},"5767":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-41","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#status","content":"decorators, prototypes and Proxy-based callable services, `this` binding;","locale":"en"},"5768":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-42","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#status","content":"waterfall and the deprecated `dispatch` API;","locale":"en"},"5769":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-43","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#status","content":"Node module resolution and loader internals (`resolve`, `import`, `create`, `package-manager`, `loader-utils`);","locale":"en"},"5770":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-44","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#status","content":"JavaScript stack frames;","locale":"en"},"5771":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-45","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#status","content":"concurrent reads of one async iterator.","locale":"en"},"5772":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-46","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#status","content":"The current workspace contains 17 library/application crates plus the unpublished `xtask`. Published crate versions are listed under Releases; the newly added HTTP/Server crates are source additions and were not released in this work.","locale":"en"},"5773":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-47","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#status","content":"CI (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`) runs `cargo fmt --check`, clippy with warnings as errors, `cargo xtask arch` and the test suite on pushes and pull requests that change Rust code, on a pinned toolchain (Rust 1.99.1), because the compile-error snapshots of `kardia-macros` change with the compiler. Pushing a `v*` tag runs `release.yml`: it reuses CI's run of the tagged commit, then runs `cargo publish --workspace` for every crate version crates.io does not have yet, waiting out its limit on new crates, and creates the GitHub release. The workflow can also be started by hand to resume a release that stopped part-way. Contributing describes the workflows step by step.","locale":"en"},"5774":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-48","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"Where the implementation departs from this plan or from the TypeScript code. Three entries are long and have their own subsections below the table.","locale":"en"},"5775":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-49","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"Topic","locale":"en"},"5776":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-50","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"Implementation","locale":"en"},"5777":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-51","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"Retired providers","locale":"en"},"5778":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-52","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"Found by the property tests: retiring a fiber unbinds its live provisions at once, so a successor can provide in the same realm while old consumers still drain (they read through their committed views). The TS code keeps the slot until the drain and fails the successor.","locale":"en"},"5779":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-53","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"Stale loads","locale":"en"},"5780":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-54","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"A retired fiber's load still in flight may register inverses (so nothing it acquired leaks), but not provisions.","locale":"en"},"5781":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-55","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"Provision removal during a drain","locale":"en"},"5782":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-56","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"Postponed to the end of the provider's drain, so descendants can reach inherited services during their own cleanup without relying on microtask order.","locale":"en"},"5783":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-57","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"Re-pointable contexts","locale":"en"},"5784":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-58","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"Scope nodes carry their fiber; the fiber chain of an access is read off the derivation chain, so moving an entry also moves the fibers below it (the TS prototype behaviour). Entry tables are mutable `Overlay` nodes.","locale":"en"},"5785":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-59","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"Checked typed access","locale":"en"},"5786":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-60","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"`Injected<D>::get::<S, _>()` (the `_` is the inferred position of `S` in `D`); an undeclared `S` does not compile.","locale":"en"},"5787":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-61","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"Expressions","locale":"en"},"5788":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-62","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"A side-effect-free JavaScript subset (`kardia_loader::expr`) over JSON views of services; no function calls.","locale":"en"},"5789":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-63","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"Errors","locale":"en"},"5790":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-64","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"Config-file errors and loader-logged entry errors carry `file:line:col` locations as text. See Errors.","locale":"en"},"5791":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-65","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"Loader API","locale":"en"},"5792":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-66","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"Resolving a parent group waits for a group entry whose plugin is still loading (its group exists only once its `apply` runs).","locale":"en"},"5793":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-67","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"Service-to-service calls","locale":"en"},"5794":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-68","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"A service resolves its own dependencies through the context it stored (its definition site), so interception metadata of the outer caller does not reach the services it calls in turn (it stays on `Svc::caller()`), and effects a service creates through its stored context belong to the service's fiber. In TS, both follow the use site.","locale":"en"},"5795":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-69","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"Events","locale":"en"},"5796":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-70","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"`emit` and `bail` log a panicking listener and continue, `serial` returns its panic as an error, `parallel` collects it (see Dispatching); TS lets a throwing listener abort the dispatch. There is no generic `waterfall`; typed hooks (`on_config`, `on_update`) replace it.","locale":"en"},"5797":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-71","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"Logger","locale":"en"},"5798":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-72","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"Messages are formatted by the caller (`impl Display`), so exporters receive finished text: no `%`-directives or custom formatters.","locale":"en"},"5799":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-73","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"Loader hooks","locale":"en"},"5800":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-74","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"`loader/patch-context` is `Loader::on_patch_context(ctx, hook)`; `EntryHandle::init`; an hmr case without a Rust counterpart. See Loader hooks.","locale":"en"},"5801":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-75","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"Model tests","locale":"en"},"5802":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-76","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"The property tests drive real tokio schedules with random delays rather than a deterministic scheduler. They check ordering during every run, and preservation, recovery exactness and confluence (the active fibers are exactly the support set) at quiescence.","locale":"en"},"5803":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-77","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"Dynamic modules","locale":"en"},"5804":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-78","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"`ModuleSource` / `ModuleGraph` / `Hmr`, three file backends (WASM, dylib, process) sharing one JSON guest ABI, the `kardia-guest` SDK and watch mode. See Dynamic modules.","locale":"en"},"5805":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-79","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#errors","content":"Parse and validation errors of config files carry `file:line:col`.","locale":"en"},"5806":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-80","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#errors","content":"Errors the loader logs for an entry (option and config validation, `ModuleNotFound`, expression failures) are located through `TreeBackend::locate` / `locate_expr`:","locale":"en"},"5807":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-81","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#errors","content":"each issue gets `(at path, file:line:col)`;","locale":"en"},"5808":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-82","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#errors","content":"an expression failure gets an `at <path> (file:line:col)` frame;","locale":"en"},"5809":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-83","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#errors","content":"the entry and each ancestor that has a position get an `at <id> (file:line:col)` frame,","locale":"en"},"5810":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-84","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#errors","content":"as text instead of rewritten JS stacks.","locale":"en"},"5811":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-85","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#errors","content":"The include locates values where their owner writes them (its file, or a patch in its parent's file), aliases at their anchors, expression chars through YAML escapes. Values changed at runtime are not located. `Entry::evaluate` is not exposed.","locale":"en"},"5812":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-86","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#loader-hooks","content":"The `loader/patch-context` waterfall is `Loader::on_patch_context(ctx, hook)`: a hook gets the entry and the rest of the chain as a future, which builds the rest only when awaited; hooks run in registration order inside the loader's own isolation step and are removed with `ctx`'s fiber. Unlike TS, the new isolation table is installed together with the re-parenting at the end of the chain, not before the hooks run.","locale":"en"},"5813":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-87","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#loader-hooks","content":"`EntryHandle::init` starts an entry or joins its pending start.","locale":"en"},"5814":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-88","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#loader-hooks","content":"The hmr `index` case \"waits for a second re-enable init\" has no hmr decision to wait on in Rust (the transaction has decided once the held unload completes); its port guards that the re-enables leave one live instance and hmr adds no apply.","locale":"en"},"5815":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-89","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#dynamic-modules","content":"`ModuleSource` / `ModuleGraph` / `Hmr`, plus three file backends that share one JSON guest ABI (documented in the `kardia-hmr` crate docs) and a bridge plugin instantiating the guest per fiber.","locale":"en"},"5816":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-90","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#dynamic-modules","content":"Backend","locale":"en"},"5817":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-91","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#dynamic-modules","content":"How it works","locale":"en"},"5818":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-92","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#dynamic-modules","content":"`WasmSource`","locale":"en"},"5819":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-93","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#dynamic-modules","content":"Runs core WASM modules on wasmtime, one store per fiber, instead of WIT components, so guests need no component toolchain (the tests are written in WAT).","locale":"en"},"5820":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-94","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#dynamic-modules","content":"`DylibSource`","locale":"en"},"5821":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-95","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#dynamic-modules","content":"Speaks a C ABI instead of passing Rust types: a cdylib carries its own copies of `kardia-core` and tokio, with separate statics and no runtime context. Each version is loaded from a unique copy and never unloaded.","locale":"en"},"5822":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-96","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#dynamic-modules","content":"`ProcessSource`","locale":"en"},"5823":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-97","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#dynamic-modules","content":"Runs executables, one child process per fiber, speaking the same ABI as JSON lines over stdin and stdout, in protocol version 1 or `kardia-guest/2` (request ids, cancellation, pings). Respawn = reload; a guest whose process ends fails its own fiber (`Fiber::fail`, with an exit report), and a guest that breaks the protocol, exceeds the frame limit or misses a call's deadline is killed. `ProcessGuest` adds arguments, a `Spawner`, timeouts and metadata given by the host. There is no separate broker service: each fiber's `GuestService` is the RPC endpoint.","locale":"en"},"5824":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-98","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#dynamic-modules","content":"`GuestService::call` blocks the caller until the guest answers; `call_async` is closer to the asynchronous RPC contract of paper §6.2: it runs off the async workers, with a deadline and cancellation, and the bridge runs `apply` and `dispose` the same way. Process guests do their I/O on threads of their own, so a deadline holds whichever thread waits. The transport traits (`GuestCode`, `GuestInstance`) are public, for a host's own transport, and so is each backend's guest code (`ProcessGuest`, `WasmGuest`), so a host can give a guest's metadata itself. An instance that fails (a trap, fuel, time or memory used up, a process that ended or was killed) fails its call with a typed `GuestFailure`, apart from the guest's own errors; the threads kardia-hmr starts catch their panics, which become the failure of the guest they serve; a guest provides only the services its metadata declares; wasmtime's process-wide trap handling on macOS (Mach ports or signals) is one setting for every engine kardia-hmr makes (Guest plugins). The `kardia` binary chooses a backend by file extension and does not load process guests.","locale":"en"},"5825":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-99","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#dynamic-modules","content":"Guest services are JSON views with JSON calls (`GuestService`). `kardia-guest` is the Rust guest SDK: a `Guest` trait and `export!`, which emits the WASM exports on wasm32 and the C symbols elsewhere, so one source builds for both backends; `serve` serves the process transport. Unlike the other crates (GPL-3.0-only), it is licensed MIT OR Apache-2.0.","locale":"en"},"5826":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-100","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#dynamic-modules","content":"Watch mode (`HmrConfig { watch: { enabled, files, debounce } }`) watches the scoped `fs.baseUrl` directory and reloads each debounce window as one batch. HMR requires FS; include scopes it to the included file’s directory. Override the directory with an FS intercept. `HmrConfig.base` and the loader tree’s independent `base_dir` interface have been removed. Failed imports retry in the next batch; host-module changes are only reported.","locale":"en"},"5827":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-101","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#dynamic-modules","content":"The `kardia` binary registers `hmr` and resolves any entry name that is not a built-in plugin as a guest module relative to the config file: `examples/kardia.yml` hot-reloads `plugins/hello.wat`.","locale":"en"},"5828":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-102","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#dynamic-modules","content":"For using guests, see Guest plugins and Hot reload.","locale":"en"},"5829":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-103","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#typescript","content":"`js/` holds the npm packages `@kardiajs/core`, `@kardiajs/timer`, `@kardiajs/bridge`, `@kardiajs/tauri` and `@kardiajs/vue`, and `crates/kardia-bridge` the Rust host of the bridge (TypeScript and Tauri). They are versioned and released apart from the crates (Contributing).","locale":"en"},"5830":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-104","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#typescript","content":"`@kardiajs/core` ports Kardia's core (its state machine, checked access, effect engine and logger), not the TypeScript Cordis code. It does not depend on Cordis, at run time or in development: this repository never did (`cordis/` is a local, gitignored copy for reference), and the packages have no runtime dependencies. Its test suite ports the relevant Cordis specs. Where it differs:","locale":"en"},"5831":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-105","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#typescript","content":"Topic","locale":"en"},"5832":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-106","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#typescript","content":"`@kardiajs/core`","locale":"en"},"5833":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-107","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#typescript","content":"Service access","locale":"en"},"5834":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-108","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#typescript","content":"`ctx.get(key)` / `ctx.live(key)` instead of property access through a `Proxy` context. A plugin defined with `definePlugin` gets a context typed by its `inject`, so an undeclared access is a type error (Kardia's `Injected<D>`).","locale":"en"},"5835":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-109","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#typescript","content":"Caller attribution","locale":"en"},"5836":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-110","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#typescript","content":"A `Service` comes back from `ctx.get` as a view whose `caller` is the accessing context (Kardia's `Svc::caller`). Views are `Proxy` objects, so a service's `#private` fields do not pass through them; use `private`.","locale":"en"},"5837":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-111","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#typescript","content":"`apply`","locale":"en"},"5838":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-112","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#typescript","content":"May return a promise, or a function that runs when the plugin stops (as in Cordis; Kardia's `apply` returns only its result). Classes are instantiated per fiber; there is no `Service.init`.","locale":"en"},"5839":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-113","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#typescript","content":"Effects","locale":"en"},"5840":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-114","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#typescript","content":"`ctx.effect(scope => ...)` with `scope.defer`, `scope.adopt` and `scope.checkpoint`, or a returned disposer (Kardia's `effect_sync` / `effect` in one). No generator or async-iterator effects.","locale":"en"},"5841":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-115","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#typescript","content":"Internal events","locale":"en"},"5842":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-116","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#typescript","content":"`internal/plugin-inserted` and `internal/plugin-removed` (Kardia's `PluginInserted` / `PluginRemoved`) instead of Cordis' single `internal/plugin`; `internal/status` passes the old and new state.","locale":"en"},"5843":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-117","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#typescript","content":"Events","locale":"en"},"5844":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-118","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#typescript","content":"As in Kardia: `emit` logs a failing listener and runs the rest; no `waterfall`; update hooks are `ctx.onUpdate`. No decorators, no `this` binding of listeners.","locale":"en"},"5845":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-119","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#typescript","content":"Recovery","locale":"en"},"5846":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-120","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#typescript","content":"`fiber.setRecovery` supports manual (default), bounded retry and asynchronous host decisions; `fiber.fail` reports instance failure. Timer callback errors are operation failures, and disposal awaits in-flight callbacks. Available in source, not yet in a new npm release; see details.","locale":"en"},"5847":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-121","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#typescript","content":"`update` / `restart`","locale":"en"},"5848":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-122","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#typescript","content":"Throw at once for an invalid config or a retired fiber (Kardia's `Result`); the promise they return, which settles with the fiber, is marked handled, so dropping it never raises an unhandled rejection.","locale":"en"},"5849":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-123","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#typescript","content":"`ctx.inject(deps, apply)`","locale":"en"},"5850":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-124","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#typescript","content":"Creates a plugin of its own on every call, so `registry.replace` cannot target it.","locale":"en"},"5851":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-125","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#typescript","content":"Names","locale":"en"},"5852":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-126","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#typescript","content":"A fiber keeps its plugin's name after its last fiber is retired, so the events of its removal still name it.","locale":"en"},"5853":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-127","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#typescript","content":"Loader, include, HMR","locale":"en"},"5854":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-128","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#typescript","content":"Not ported: they read files, and the client runs in a webview. Vite's HMR can drive `registry.replace`.","locale":"en"},"5855":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-129","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#typescript","content":"The bridge's calls are asynchronous, unlike the guest ABI's (Dynamic modules): it is the asynchronous contract of paper §6.2. Its end-to-end tests drive the real Rust host from the TypeScript client.","locale":"en"},"5856":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","type":"page","content":"Testing strategy","breadcrumbs":["kardia Documentation","Development"],"tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-testing","locale":"en"},"5857":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-0","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing","content":"How kardia is tested: the ported TypeScript spec suite and its runtimes, model-based property tests of the paper's metatheory, unit tests and tooling.","locale":"en"},"5858":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-1","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-testing#port-the-suite","content":"Port the TypeScript suite (acceptance)","locale":"en"},"5859":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-2","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-testing#model-based","content":"Model-based tests of the paper's metatheory","locale":"en"},"5860":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-3","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-testing#unit-tests","content":"Unit tests","locale":"en"},"5861":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-4","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-testing#tooling","content":"Tooling","locale":"en"},"5862":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-5","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-testing#server","content":"Server parity and failure tests","locale":"en"},"5863":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-6","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-testing#js-recovery","content":"JS recovery and cross-runtime isolation","locale":"en"},"5864":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-7","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing","content":"kardia's tests check the implementation against two specifications: the TypeScript test suite of Cordis (behaviour) and the paper's theorems (semantics). The tests exercise the real implementation; mocks are kept to a minimum.","locale":"en"},"5865":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-8","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#port-the-suite","content":"Each phase ports the corresponding `*.spec.ts` files as Rust integration tests, keeping the snake_case of each TS test name for traceability. The runtime follows what a test exercises:","locale":"en"},"5866":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-9","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#port-the-suite","content":"Runtime","locale":"en"},"5867":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-10","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#port-the-suite","content":"Used for","locale":"en"},"5868":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-11","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#port-the-suite","content":"**`current_thread`** (`#[tokio::test]`)","locale":"en"},"5869":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-12","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#port-the-suite","content":"The default: core, loader and hmr tests. Interleavings stay close to the single JS thread.","locale":"en"},"5870":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-13","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#port-the-suite","content":"**Paused time** (`start_paused = true`)","locale":"en"},"5871":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-14","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#port-the-suite","content":"Tests that depend on timers: the timer suite, and the core and loader tests whose effects wait on timers.","locale":"en"},"5872":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-15","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#port-the-suite","content":"**`multi_thread`**","locale":"en"},"5873":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-16","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#port-the-suite","content":"Where real parallelism is the point: every include test, plus the fs and hmr watch cases that need it (e.g. a module load held open with `block_in_place`). The include's IO loop and the file watch then run alongside tree updates, as in the `kardia` binary. These tests found the races fixed in the include's write path and the loader's update path.","locale":"en"},"5874":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-17","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#port-the-suite","content":"**Real time**","locale":"en"},"5875":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-18","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#port-the-suite","content":"File-system watch tests, since `notify` reports events from its own thread. An expected event is awaited with bounded polling (`eventually` helpers); a fixed wait is used only to check that something does *not* happen.","locale":"en"},"5876":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-19","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#port-the-suite","content":"The specs per phase (the phases are on Status and deviations):","locale":"en"},"5877":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-20","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#port-the-suite","content":"Phase","locale":"en"},"5878":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-21","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#port-the-suite","content":"Specs to port","locale":"en"},"5879":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-22","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#port-the-suite","content":"1","locale":"en"},"5880":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-23","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#port-the-suite","content":"`dispose.spec` (effect shapes, abort at boundaries, finally/cleanup, errors)","locale":"en"},"5881":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-24","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#port-the-suite","content":"2","locale":"en"},"5882":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-25","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#port-the-suite","content":"`fiber.spec` (inertia locks 1–3, failure, update, dispose of pending/failed), `update.spec` (concurrent updates), `service.spec`, `service-dispose.spec` (guards, drain ordering, concurrent sibling cleanup), `plugin.spec`, `replace.spec`, `isolate.spec`, `reflect.spec`","locale":"en"},"5883":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-26","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#port-the-suite","content":"3","locale":"en"},"5884":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-27","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#port-the-suite","content":"`events.spec`, `intercept.spec`, `logger.spec`, `shadow.spec` (re-expressed as def-site/use-site tests on `Svc`), timer `index.spec`","locale":"en"},"5885":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-28","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#port-the-suite","content":"4","locale":"en"},"5886":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-29","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#port-the-suite","content":"loader `validate.spec`, `commit.spec`, `group.spec`, `index.spec`, `evaluate.spec`, `rebuild.spec`, `isolate*.spec`","locale":"en"},"5887":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-30","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#port-the-suite","content":"5","locale":"en"},"5888":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-31","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#port-the-suite","content":"include `sync.spec`, `patch.spec`, `reload.spec`, `write.spec` (fault injection on rename), `parse.spec`; fs `watch.spec`","locale":"en"},"5889":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-32","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#port-the-suite","content":"7","locale":"en"},"5890":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-33","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#port-the-suite","content":"hmr `reconcile.spec` (with an in-memory `ModuleSource`)","locale":"en"},"5891":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-34","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#port-the-suite","content":"Every case of every spec has been ported, found already covered, or judged not applicable; see Implementation status.","locale":"en"},"5892":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-35","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#model-based","content":"Because the lifecycle is written as pure critical sections (Concurrency), the core can be driven by a **deterministic scheduler** in tests: a harness that holds spawned transitions in a queue and lets `proptest` choose which runnable task advances next. With synthetic components whose effects are writes to a shared table (each with an exact inverse), random orchestration sequences (insert / retire / provide / withdraw / update) and random schedules check the theorems of the metatheory:","locale":"en"},"5893":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-36","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#model-based","content":"Property","locale":"en"},"5894":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-37","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#model-based","content":"Theorem","locale":"en"},"5895":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-38","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#model-based","content":"Checked as","locale":"en"},"5896":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-39","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#model-based","content":"**Preservation**","locale":"en"},"5897":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-40","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#model-based","content":"Thm 64","locale":"en"},"5898":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-41","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#model-based","content":"Registry well-formedness after every step (parents exist, provisions disjoint per realm, committed views name installed fibers).","locale":"en"},"5899":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-42","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#model-based","content":"**Recovery exactness**","locale":"en"},"5900":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-43","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#model-based","content":"Thm 68 / Cor 69","locale":"en"},"5901":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-44","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#model-based","content":"After a fiber's episode closes, the table equals the run with that fiber's steps removed; a removed fiber's provisions are empty.","locale":"en"},"5902":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-45","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#model-based","content":"**Ordering**","locale":"en"},"5903":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-46","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#model-based","content":"Thm 70","locale":"en"},"5904":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-47","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#model-based","content":"A provider's episode strictly contains each consumer's episode that committed to it; no consumer reads a binding outside its provider's episode.","locale":"en"},"5905":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-48","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#model-based","content":"**Resolution coherence**","locale":"en"},"5906":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-49","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#model-based","content":"Thm 71","locale":"en"},"5907":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-50","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#model-based","content":"Every `apply` observes one committed view.","locale":"en"},"5908":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-51","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#model-based","content":"**Progress**","locale":"en"},"5909":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-52","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#model-based","content":"Thm 73","locale":"en"},"5910":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-53","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#model-based","content":"Every schedule reaches quiescence within the step bound (acyclic declarations).","locale":"en"},"5911":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-54","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#model-based","content":"**Confluence**","locale":"en"},"5912":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-55","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#model-based","content":"Thm 80","locale":"en"},"5913":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-56","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#model-based","content":"For fixed orchestration inputs, all schedules reach the same set of `Active` fibers (= the support set A) and the same table up to fiber renaming.","locale":"en"},"5914":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-57","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#model-based","content":"These tests check the implementation against the theory with no business logic copied into the tests.","locale":"en"},"5915":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-58","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#model-based","content":"As implemented (`crates/kardia-core/tests/model.rs`), the property tests drive real tokio schedules with random delays rather than a deterministic scheduler. They check ordering during every run, and preservation, recovery exactness and confluence (the active fibers are exactly the support set) at quiescence. The property tests also found the retired-provider deviation.","locale":"en"},"5916":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-59","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#unit-tests","content":"Pure modules (`IdTable`, `Scope` lookups, target computation, `journal`, `patch`, glob predicate, HMR union-find) get focused unit tests. Mocks are limited to `Catalog`/`ModuleSource` test registries and fault-injecting filesystem shims.","locale":"en"},"5917":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-60","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#tooling","content":"`cargo fmt`, `cargo clippy -D warnings`, `cargo test --workspace` (the exact CI commands are on Contributing).","locale":"en"},"5918":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-61","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#tooling","content":"`unsafe` is confined to the FFI of the dylib backend (`kardia-hmr/src/dylib.rs`) and the guest SDK (`kardia-guest/src/ffi.rs`); every other crate forbids it.","locale":"en"},"5919":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-62","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#tooling","content":"Optional `loom` is *not* planned: the single-lock design has no lock-free state to model.","locale":"en"},"5920":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-63","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#server","content":"The five Server packages have 105 original upstream declarations and 201 real supplement/dual-host tests on Linux, Windows and macOS. This includes full MIME fixtures, TLS, WS, CLI integration and all three recovery policies while unrelated plugins continue working. See commands and evidence; regenerate inventory/MIME sources with the existing scripts, never by hand.","locale":"en"},"5921":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-64","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#js-recovery","content":"`js.yml` runs package type checks, tests, builds and real Rust subprocess bridge E2E on Linux, Windows and macOS. It verifies recovery in each runtime independently of the manual upstream Server comparison workflow.","locale":"en"},"5922":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-65","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#js-recovery","content":"Local acceptance passed 155 tests in 14 JS files, 10 real cross-runtime E2E tests, and 11 Rust bridge tests plus clippy, fmt and architecture checks. New coverage includes:","locale":"en"},"5923":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-66","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#js-recovery","content":"17 core tests: repeated failure under all three policies, recovery after cleanup, independent plugin progress, default manual handling, failed loads, no unchanged validation retries, throwing/rejecting host handlers, and cancellation on policy changes or removal.","locale":"en"},"5924":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-67","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#js-recovery","content":"15 timer tests: synchronous/asynchronous errors across all four schedulers, caller attribution and awaiting callbacks on disposal; repeated explicit instance failures under all three policies.","locale":"en"},"5925":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-68","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#js-recovery","content":"5 additional bridge tests: three manual/retry/host recovery rounds in a real Rust provider, JS consumer withdrawal/recreation, uninterrupted independent Rust/JS calls and timer progress without restarting the bridge, plus removal cancelling retry/host recovery.","locale":"en"},"5926":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-69","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#js-recovery","content":"Some existing unit tests use fake timers; the new fault tests use real timers and implementations, and E2E uses real subprocess communication. Native CI does not constitute independent testing on the user's devices, in a browser or in a Tauri webview. See policies and same-thread boundaries.","locale":"en"},"5927":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-70","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#js-recovery","content":"Native acceptance: all six jobs in JS #43707771734 passed at `3b67c40` (155 package tests, five package builds and 10 E2E tests on each platform). All three jobs in Rust CI #43707335399 passed at `fd1a6e3`; subsequent changes only touched JS supervision, tests and documentation, leaving Rust source unchanged. Site #43707771760 passed type checks, 41 tests, the full build and publishing. No versions were bumped or npm/crates packages published in this work.","locale":"en"},"5928":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-71","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#js-recovery","content":"Native validation found Windows LICENSE copying failed because the existing build script concatenated path separators; native paths and a portable compiler invocation fixed it. The E2E harness now drains shutdown requests to avoid Windows EPIPE, and observes connection events after initial readiness to exclude macOS startup retries. Assertions for no reconnection during failures, independent progress and cleanup remain intact. Cancelled intermediate runs are not acceptance evidence.","locale":"en"},"5929":{"id":"/en/docs/development","page_id":"/en/docs/development","type":"page","content":"Rust design","breadcrumbs":["kardia Documentation","Development"],"tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development","locale":"en"},"5930":{"id":"/en/docs/development-0","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development","content":"The design of kardia, the Rust implementation of Cordis: goals, the concept mapping from the paper to Rust, and the crate layout.","locale":"en"},"5931":{"id":"/en/docs/development-1","page_id":"/en/docs/development","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development#goals","content":"Goals, non-goals, guiding decisions","locale":"en"},"5932":{"id":"/en/docs/development-2","page_id":"/en/docs/development","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development#goals-list","content":"Goals","locale":"en"},"5933":{"id":"/en/docs/development-3","page_id":"/en/docs/development","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development#non-goals","content":"Non-goals (for the first releases)","locale":"en"},"5934":{"id":"/en/docs/development-4","page_id":"/en/docs/development","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development#decisions","content":"Guiding decisions","locale":"en"},"5935":{"id":"/en/docs/development-5","page_id":"/en/docs/development","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development#concept-mapping","content":"Concept mapping: paper → Cordis (TS) → Rust","locale":"en"},"5936":{"id":"/en/docs/development-6","page_id":"/en/docs/development","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development#layout","content":"Workspace layout","locale":"en"},"5937":{"id":"/en/docs/development-7","page_id":"/en/docs/development","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development#dependencies","content":"Dependency direction","locale":"en"},"5938":{"id":"/en/docs/development-8","page_id":"/en/docs/development","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development#external-dependencies","content":"External dependencies","locale":"en"},"5939":{"id":"/en/docs/development-9","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development","content":"This section is the design of kardia, the Rust re-implementation of the Cordis framework. It builds on","locale":"en"},"5940":{"id":"/en/docs/development-10","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development","content":"the **theory**: the Concepts section (the paper's definitions, rules and theorems), and","locale":"en"},"5941":{"id":"/en/docs/development-11","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development","content":"the **reference implementation**: the Cordis reference section (TypeScript, Cordis v4),","locale":"en"},"5942":{"id":"/en/docs/development-12","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development","content":"and is explicit about which of the two it follows where they differ (see Paper vs. code). The design is implemented in `crates/`; Status and deviations records the status and where the implementation departs from this text.","locale":"en"},"5943":{"id":"/en/docs/development-13","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development","content":"The section started as a plan written before the code. Its text is kept as the record of the design. Where the code in `crates/` now differs from a signature or sketch, a note below the sketch says so, and Status and deviations lists the deliberate departures.","locale":"en"},"5944":{"id":"/en/docs/development-14","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development","content":"<Card title=\"Concurrency\" href=\"/docs/development-concurrency\" description=\"One lock per root, critical sections that return actions, lock ordering.\" />","locale":"en"},"5945":{"id":"/en/docs/development-15","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development","content":"<Card title=\"Core data structures\" href=\"/docs/development-lifecycle\" description=\"Identifiers, scopes, bindings, fibers, IdTable, cycle detection.\" />","locale":"en"},"5946":{"id":"/en/docs/development-16","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development","content":"<Card title=\"API design\" href=\"/docs/development-design\" description=\"Services and access, effects, plugins and configuration, events and logger.\" />","locale":"en"},"5947":{"id":"/en/docs/development-17","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development","content":"<Card title=\"Loader internals\" href=\"/docs/development-loader\" description=\"kardia-loader, kardia-include, dynamic modules and HMR.\" />","locale":"en"},"5948":{"id":"/en/docs/development-18","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development","content":"<Card title=\"Testing\" href=\"/docs/development-testing\" description=\"Ported TypeScript specs, model-based tests of the metatheory, tooling.\" />","locale":"en"},"5949":{"id":"/en/docs/development-19","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development","content":"<Card title=\"Status and deviations\" href=\"/docs/development-status\" description=\"Implementation phases, status, and every departure from the plan.\" />","locale":"en"},"5950":{"id":"/en/docs/development-20","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development","content":"<Card title=\"Risks\" href=\"/docs/development-risks\" description=\"Risks and open decisions, and a worked example of the API.\" />","locale":"en"},"5951":{"id":"/en/docs/development-21","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development","content":"<Card title=\"Contributing\" href=\"/docs/development-contributing\" description=\"Building, testing, CI, releases and this documentation site.\" />","locale":"en"},"5952":{"id":"/en/docs/development-22","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development","content":"<Card title=\"Server architecture and acceptance\" href=\"/docs/development-server\" description=\"Ownership, recovery, upstream comparisons and native CI evidence.\" />","locale":"en"},"5953":{"id":"/en/docs/development-23","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development#goals-list","content":"**Semantic fidelity to the calculus** (paper §4): fibers, target vs. committed views, the guard on unload, inertia, failure outcomes, single source per (key, realm), confluence.","locale":"en"},"5954":{"id":"/en/docs/development-24","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development#goals-list","content":"**Behavioural parity with Cordis core** where the paper is silent (epoch with revision, two-level guard, concurrent fiber-level disposal, `update` veto, `replace`), using the TypeScript test suite as the acceptance specification.","locale":"en"},"5955":{"id":"/en/docs/development-25","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development#goals-list","content":"**Idiomatic Rust**: typed service keys, explicit caller attribution instead of `Proxy`, `Send + Sync` handles usable from a multi-threaded tokio runtime, no `unsafe` in the core.","locale":"en"},"5956":{"id":"/en/docs/development-26","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development#goals-list","content":"A declarative **loader** (entries, groups, realms) and file-backed **include** with two-way sync.","locale":"en"},"5957":{"id":"/en/docs/development-27","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development#non-goals","content":"Re-evaluating plugin *code* at runtime (HMR). Rust has no module registry; this becomes an optional, later \"module source\" extension (Loader internals).","locale":"en"},"5958":{"id":"/en/docs/development-28","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development#non-goals","content":"npm-specific tooling (`package-manager`, `create`, `loader-utils`). Not ported.","locale":"en"},"5959":{"id":"/en/docs/development-29","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development#non-goals","content":"Byte-exact parity of error messages and UTF-16 column numbers (nice-to-have, not required).","locale":"en"},"5960":{"id":"/en/docs/development-30","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development#non-goals","content":"HMR was a non-goal for the first releases only. It has since been implemented in `kardia-hmr` (WASM, dylib and process backends, watch mode); see Status and deviations.","locale":"en"},"5961":{"id":"/en/docs/development-31","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development#decisions","content":"A summary; each decision is justified on the page linked in the last column.","locale":"en"},"5962":{"id":"/en/docs/development-32","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development#decisions","content":"#","locale":"en"},"5963":{"id":"/en/docs/development-33","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development#decisions","content":"Decision","locale":"en"},"5964":{"id":"/en/docs/development-34","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development#decisions","content":"Section","locale":"en"},"5965":{"id":"/en/docs/development-35","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development#decisions","content":"D1","locale":"en"},"5966":{"id":"/en/docs/development-36","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development#decisions","content":"`Send + Sync` core; one state lock per root; **no user code runs while the lock is held**; every JS \"synchronous segment\" becomes one critical section; follow-up work is returned as an action list and executed after unlocking","locale":"en"},"5967":{"id":"/en/docs/development-37","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development#decisions","content":"Concurrency","locale":"en"},"5968":{"id":"/en/docs/development-38","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development#decisions","content":"D2","locale":"en"},"5969":{"id":"/en/docs/development-39","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development#decisions","content":"Fibers live in a registry arena addressed by never-reused `FiberId`s; all cross references (parent, dependents, committed view) are ids, never pointers","locale":"en"},"5970":{"id":"/en/docs/development-40","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development#decisions","content":"Core data structures","locale":"en"},"5971":{"id":"/en/docs/development-41","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development#decisions","content":"D3","locale":"en"},"5972":{"id":"/en/docs/development-42","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development#decisions","content":"Service keys are **strings** (needed by configuration) bound to a **Rust type** via a `Service` trait; values stored as `Arc<dyn Any + Send + Sync>` plus their `TypeId` (detects key collisions)","locale":"en"},"5973":{"id":"/en/docs/development-43","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/d