espondences","content":"`resolveIntercept`: declared `inject[name]` ⊕ outer→inner context intercepts, right-biased","locale":"en"},"8689":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-80","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#correspondences","content":"Committed-view access","locale":"en"},"8690":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-81","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#correspondences","content":"Algorithm 6","locale":"en"},"8691":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-82","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#correspondences","content":"Proxy `get` → `locate`","locale":"en"},"8692":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-83","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#correspondences","content":"plus own provisions and isolation boundaries","locale":"en"},"8693":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-84","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#correspondences","content":"Entry fields","locale":"en"},"8694":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-85","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#correspondences","content":"Def. 81","locale":"en"},"8695":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-86","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#correspondences","content":"`EntryOptions`","locale":"en"},"8696":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-87","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#correspondences","content":"adds `inject`, `group`","locale":"en"},"8697":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-88","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#correspondences","content":"Managed realms + delimiters","locale":"en"},"8698":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-89","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#correspondences","content":"Algorithm 7","locale":"en"},"8699":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-90","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#correspondences","content":"`loader/src/config/isolate.ts`","locale":"en"},"8700":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-91","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#correspondences","content":"plus migration of the provider's own impl without restart","locale":"en"},"8701":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-92","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#target","content":"**Paper:** `fiber.target` is a hash of `target(γ, n)` (key → provider uid tuple), ⊥ when unsatisfied.","locale":"en"},"8702":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-93","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#target","content":"**Code:** `_runner.epoch` = `\"<revision>:<uid>:<uid>…\"` or `'__INACTIVE__'`. The **revision** counter folds configuration revisions into the target: `restart()`, `update()` and `registry.replace()` bump it, forcing an unload→reload chain without retiring the fiber. This realizes the §4.4 *Configuration* revision more cheaply than retire → remove → reinsert at the same name.","locale":"en"},"8703":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-94","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#target","content":"See Target computation.","locale":"en"},"8704":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-95","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#retirement","content":"**Paper:** O-Retire (set τ) and O-Remove (drop the entry) are separate rules; a retired entry may linger (vestigial).","locale":"en"},"8705":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-96","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#retirement","content":"**Code:** the instantiation effect's inverse sets `uid = null` (removed from the runtime immediately), forces the target to ⊥, then awaits the transition chain; the final state is `DISPOSED`. There is no persistent registry of retired entries. Re-enabling an entry creates a **fresh fiber** (as the paper prescribes).","locale":"en"},"8706":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-97","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#retirement","content":"See Instantiation.","locale":"en"},"8707":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-98","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#guard","content":"**Paper (Algorithm 5):** `unload` awaits the `notify(...)`'d dependents' `await()`.","locale":"en"},"8708":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-99","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#guard","content":"**Code:** explicit reverse references `Impl.dependents: Set<Fiber>`, populated at commit time and removed at the end of the dependent's unload, plus wake-up resolvers (`impl.release`, `fiber._release`). Two guards exist: the **fiber-level** guard (no disposer of the provider runs until *all* its provisions are free) and the **binding-level** guard inside `provide`'s inverse. This gives the paper's per-binding semantics (`relied_n`) while letting a provider withdraw one binding without waiting for another.","locale":"en"},"8709":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-100","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#guard","content":"See The two guards.","locale":"en"},"8710":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-101","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#accumulator","content":"**Paper:** the accumulator is a LIFO composite; Algorithm 5 runs `fiber.dispose()` once.","locale":"en"},"8711":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-102","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#accumulator","content":"**Code:** fiber-level disposers are *initiated* in reverse order but run **concurrently** (`Promise.all`), each after one microtask; errors are logged and do not stop the others. Sequential LIFO holds only *inside* one `ctx.effect`. The paper acknowledges the concurrency (§5.1.3) and it is justified by Theorem 43 (independent inverses commute), but it means ordering-sensitive teardown must be grouped into one effect.","locale":"en"},"8712":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-103","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#accumulator","content":"See Fiber-level accumulator.","locale":"en"},"8713":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-104","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#iteration-boundaries","content":"**Paper:** the guard is checked before each iteration; L-Divert may abort or land.","locale":"en"},"8714":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-105","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#iteration-boundaries","content":"**Code:** only **async** iterators are interruptible; sync iterators and plain functions run to completion (they cannot be interrupted in a single-threaded host anyway). Async iterators take the *landing* alternative (an inertial host) and are closed with `iter.return()` so `finally` blocks run. `_reload` also yields one microtask before executing, so superseded loads never start.","locale":"en"},"8715":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-106","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#notification","content":"**Paper:** `notify` iterates all fibers and calls `refresh`.","locale":"en"},"8716":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-107","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#notification","content":"**Code:** it iterates all runtimes' fibers, filters by declared key and **realm symbol** (or a custom filter during realm moves), recomputes only the affected keys (`_checkImpl`), then calls `_refresh`. It also emits `internal/service`. Notifications for a fiber's provisions are issued from `_updateState` only on transitions into or out of `ACTIVE`.","locale":"en"},"8717":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-108","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#notification","content":"See `notify`.","locale":"en"},"8718":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-109","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#availability","content":"The code adds `Impl.check` (from `Service[symbols.check]`): a provider may report itself unavailable even while `ACTIVE` (used by `loader` for `{ await: true }`). This extends the satisfaction predicate `σ ⊧ d` with a per-provider condition.","locale":"en"},"8719":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-110","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#access","content":"`locate` additionally (a) lets a fiber read its **own** provisions without declaring them and (b) stops inheritance at an **isolation boundary**. The root fiber (no runtime) gets *unchecked* access (`reflect.get(prop, false)`), because root code cannot declare `inject`. See `locate`.","locale":"en"},"8720":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-111","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#caller-attribution","content":"The tracker/shadow proxy mechanism (Services and traceability) attributes effects created inside service methods to the **calling** fiber and resolves the service's own dependencies from its **def site**. The paper's confinement (Def. 55) implicitly assumes this; without it, service-mediated effects would belong to the provider and break temporal composability for consumers.","locale":"en"},"8721":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-112","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#loader-specifics","content":"See Loader.","locale":"en"},"8722":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-113","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#loader-specifics","content":"Two fibers per entry with `inject` (an outer dependency holder + the inner plugin).","locale":"en"},"8723":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-114","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#loader-specifics","content":"Expressions (`!!js`) evaluated in specific contexts; a cached `disabled` verdict.","locale":"en"},"8724":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-115","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#loader-specifics","content":"Self-dispose detection → persisted `disabled: true`; self-update → persisted config (`internal/commit`).","locale":"en"},"8725":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-116","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#loader-specifics","content":"The `loader { await: true }` barrier.","locale":"en"},"8726":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-117","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#loader-specifics","content":"Realm migration without restarting the provider; blocked moves tracked via `Impl.isolate`.","locale":"en"},"8727":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-118","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#hmr","content":"See HMR: Paper vs. code: registry-level in-place replacement (`registry.replace` + `_replace` + revision bump) instead of dispose + `ctx.use`; union-find commit units instead of all-or-nothing; no rollback after the point of no return.","locale":"en"},"8728":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-119","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#unchecked","content":"Assumption","locale":"en"},"8729":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-120","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#unchecked","content":"Status in the code","locale":"en"},"8730":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-121","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#unchecked","content":"Effect witnesses (the inverse really reverts)","locale":"en"},"8731":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-122","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#unchecked","content":"unchecked, an author obligation (stated in the paper)","locale":"en"},"8732":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-123","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#unchecked","content":"Coeffect commutativity","locale":"en"},"8733":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-124","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#unchecked","content":"unchecked, a provider obligation (stated in the paper)","locale":"en"},"8734":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-125","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#unchecked","content":"Acyclicity of ≺","locale":"en"},"8735":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-126","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#unchecked","content":"**not detected**: a dependency cycle leaves fibers PENDING silently; the paper (§6.5) says a runtime *can* report it at load time, but Cordis does not","locale":"en"},"8736":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-127","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#unchecked","content":"Totality on provision (Def. 76)","locale":"en"},"8737":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-128","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#unchecked","content":"not enforced; `provide` metadata on plugins is declarative only","locale":"en"},"8738":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-129","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#unchecked","content":"Confinement (Def. 55)","locale":"en"},"8739":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-130","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#unchecked","content":"enforced only for coeffect *access* through the proxy; arbitrary JS can still reach globals","locale":"en"},"8740":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-131","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#defects","content":"Location","locale":"en"},"8741":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-132","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#defects","content":"Observation","locale":"en"},"8742":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-133","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#defects","content":"`loader/src/config/isolate.ts:150-153`","locale":"en"},"8743":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-134","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#defects","content":"`return` inside the realm-GC scan exits the whole listener; GC for the remaining names of that entry's `legacy.isolate` is skipped. `continue` of the outer loop was likely intended.","locale":"en"},"8744":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-135","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#defects","content":"`create/src/index.ts`","locale":"en"},"8745":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-136","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#defects","content":"`--mirror` flag parsed but unused.","locale":"en"},"8746":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-137","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#rust","content":"Follow the **code's** state machine (epoch with revision, inertia, two-level guard, `FAILED`, `DISPOSED`), because it is tested and handles configuration revisions; keep the paper's names in the docs.","locale":"en"},"8747":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-138","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#rust","content":"Follow the **paper** where the code is constrained by JS: make iteration boundaries explicit for all multi-step effects; detect dependency cycles at insertion time (cheap, since declarations are static).","locale":"en"},"8748":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-139","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#rust","content":"Replace Proxy-based caller attribution with explicit typed handles.","locale":"en"},"8749":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-140","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#rust","content":"Follow the **code's** HMR design (unit-wise commit, in-place replacement) over Algorithm 10.","locale":"en"},"8750":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-141","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#rust","content":"Fix the realm-GC early `return` in the port.","locale":"en"},"8751":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-142","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#rust","content":"How kardia carries these out is described in Lifecycle, API design, Loader, include and HMR and, with every deviation listed, Status.","locale":"en"},"8752":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","type":"page","content":"Server behavior reference","breadcrumbs":["kardia Documentation","Cordis reference"],"tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/reference-server","locale":"en"},"8753":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-0","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-server","content":"HTTP, WS, ACL, Static, Proxy and auxiliary-service contracts at the fixed Cordis baseline.","locale":"en"},"8754":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-1","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/reference-server#core","content":"Core API","locale":"en"},"8755":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-2","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/reference-server#http","content":"Requests, responses and hooks","locale":"en"},"8756":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-3","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/reference-server#websocket","content":"WebSocket and Echo","locale":"en"},"8757":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-4","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/reference-server#acl","content":"ACL","locale":"en"},"8758":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-5","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/reference-server#static","content":"Static","locale":"en"},"8759":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-6","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/reference-server#proxy","content":"Proxy","locale":"en"},"8760":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-7","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/reference-server#auxiliary","content":"Auxiliary-service scope","locale":"en"},"8761":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-8","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-server","content":"This reference targets Cordis 36dc7fb5: core 1.7.0, ACL 1.0.1, Echo 1.1.1, Static 1.2.1 and Proxy 1.2.1. Expectations do not follow a floating main branch or npm version.","locale":"en"},"8762":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-9","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-server#core","content":"`kardia::server` exports `ServerPlugin`, `ServerConfig`, `Server`, `ServerExt`, `Request`, `Response`, `Body`, `Path`, `WsExt` and related types. Its service key is `server`.","locale":"en"},"8763":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-10","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-server#core","content":"Setting","locale":"en"},"8764":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-11","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-server#core","content":"Behavior","locale":"en"},"8765":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-12","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-server#core","content":"`host`","locale":"en"},"8766":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-13","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-server#core","content":"Defaults to `127.0.0.1`","locale":"en"},"8767":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-14","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-server#core","content":"`port`","locale":"en"},"8768":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-15","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-server#core","content":"Required; `0` lets the system allocate a port","locale":"en"},"8769":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-16","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-server#core","content":"`maxPort`","locale":"en"},"8770":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-17","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-server#core","content":"Defaults to the starting port; only address-in-use/permission errors advance the port","locale":"en"},"8771":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-18","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-server#core","content":"`publicUrl`","locale":"en"},"8772":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-19","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-server#core","content":"Removes trailing slashes, then appends the caller's `intercept.server.path`","locale":"en"},"8773":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-20","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-server#core","content":"Automatic URLs use the actual bound port; wildcard addresses map to `127.0.0.1`. Ports 80/443 produce http/https URLs, but the URL scheme **does not enable listener TLS**. The server uses HTTP/1; a fronting host can terminate TLS. The HTTP client supports HTTPS and WSS.","locale":"en"},"8774":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-21","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-server#core","content":"`get`, `post` and `all` are convenience registrations; `route(method, path, handler, options)` also supports DELETE, HEAD, PUT and PATCH. Routes run in registration order. GET does not imply HEAD. No matching path produces 404; a method mismatch produces 405; OPTIONS produces 204. `Allow` uses lowercase methods in registration order, with `*` for all.","locale":"en"},"8775":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-22","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-server#core","content":"String paths support parameters, wildcards, optional groups and quoted parameter names, following path-to-regexp 8.4.2. Path captures decode percent escapes and preserve literal `+`. Queries preserve duplicate keys and still decode `+` as a space. `Path::regex(source, flags)` uses ECMAScript syntax with g/y state, UTF-16 matching and Unicode flags. Intercept prefixes use string-prefix matching without an extra segment-boundary rule.","locale":"en"},"8776":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-23","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-server#core","content":"For string HTTP routes, `intercept.server.routes[\"METHOD path\"]` overrides supplied options while retaining unspecified fields. WS and regex routes do not use this override table.","locale":"en"},"8777":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-24","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-server#http","content":"`use_middleware`, `on_route_request` and `Next` form asynchronous chains with short-circuiting. `on_route_check` returns true to skip a route and is shared by HTTP/WS.","locale":"en"},"8778":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-25","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-server#http","content":"`Request` exposes method, url, path, query, headers, params and bytes/text/json/blob/form_data readers. Its body is consumed once.","locale":"en"},"8779":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-26","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-server#http","content":"`Response` supports status, headers and bytes/text/json/Blob/FormData/stream bodies. Setting status or body claims a response; setting a header alone does not. Later body writes replace earlier ones.","locale":"en"},"8780":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-27","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-server#http","content":"Ordinary errors and unwind panics produce 500 while unclaimed; validator `ValidationError` produces 422 with issues. Claimed status and content are retained.","locale":"en"},"8781":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-28","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-server#http","content":"Adopting `Some(Response)` copies body, status and headers, but **does not copy custom status text** and keeps only the last repeated Set-Cookie, matching upstream. Setting status text directly on the supplied response works.","locale":"en"},"8782":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-29","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-server#http","content":"`accepts` uses the fixed upstream MIME database and negotiation priorities. JSON output uses JS Number formatting and integer-property ordering. Send integers beyond JS Number precision as strings when exactness matters.","locale":"en"},"8783":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-30","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-server#http","content":"Rust APIs use UTF-8 strings, serde JSON values and typed callbacks. They do not emulate JS prototypes, callable objects or isolated UTF-16 surrogates. The Rust JSON value representation is not a complete JS Number runtime; response encoding follows the tested contract.","locale":"en"},"8784":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-31","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-server#websocket","content":"`ws(path, handler, options)` registers a route. An omitted handler accepts automatically; an explicit handler must call `accept.accept().await`. Returning without accepting disconnects. Errors before acceptance produce 500; errors afterwards close the connection. An unmatched WS path produces 404.","locale":"en"},"8785":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-32","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-server#websocket","content":"Valid handshakes accept versions 13/8 and select the first requested subprotocol. Invalid method/key/version/protocol status, body and headers are compared with upstream. The server message limit is 100 MiB. `WebSocket::send/recv/close` uses typed Message values, preserving text/binary distinctions and close reasons. Route disposal closes its own connections and reclaims their tasks.","locale":"en"},"8786":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-33","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-server#websocket","content":"Echo registers empty-path POST and WS routes, mounted through interception. HTTP streams the body and content-type; WS preserves message type and bytes.","locale":"en"},"8787":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-34","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-server#acl","content":"`AclPlugin` / `AclConfig` accepts `allowedHosts`: omitted or true allows all; false permits loopback only; a list additionally permits its domains. Matching ignores case and ports, recognizes bracketed IPv6, and lets `.example.com` match both the apex and subdomains. Route options replace the component's allowedHosts rather than merging lists.","locale":"en"},"8788":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-35","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-server#acl","content":"A rejection continues to later routes. If all reject, the result is 404, without changing it to 403 or revealing rejected methods through Allow. HTTP and WS share this rule.","locale":"en"},"8789":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-36","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-server#static","content":"`StaticPlugin` / `StaticConfig` depends on `server` and `fs`. It reads through providers rather than bypassing the file service for direct disk access.","locale":"en"},"8790":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-37","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-server#static","content":"Setting","locale":"en"},"8791":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-38","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-server#static","content":"Default and behavior","locale":"en"},"8792":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-39","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-server#static","content":"`root`","locale":"en"},"8793":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-40","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-server#static","content":"Required; resolved as a directory relative to fs.baseUrl, clearing query and fragment","locale":"en"},"8794":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-41","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-server#static","content":"`index`","locale":"en"},"8795":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-42","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-server#static","content":"`[\"index.html\", \"index.htm\"]`, tried in order","locale":"en"},"8796":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-43","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-server#static","content":"`redirect`","locale":"en"},"8797":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-44","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-server#static","content":"true; a directory without a trailing slash redirects with 301","locale":"en"},"8798":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-45","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-server#static","content":"`extensions`","locale":"en"},"8799":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-46","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-server#static","content":"Empty; the original file wins before ordered extension candidates","locale":"en"},"8800":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-47","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-server#static","content":"`exclude`","locale":"en"},"8801":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-48","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-server#static","content":"Empty; matches relative URL paths, including dotfiles","locale":"en"},"8802":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-49","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-server#static","content":"`fallthrough`","locale":"en"},"8803":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-50","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-server#static","content":"false; when enabled, only 404 continues","locale":"en"},"8804":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-51","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-server#static","content":"`disposition`","locale":"en"},"8805":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-52","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-server#static","content":"Unset; inline / attachment are supported","locale":"en"},"8806":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-53","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-server#static","content":"`errorPages`","locale":"en"},"8807":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-54","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-server#static","content":"Empty; selects a page by status code and retains that status","locale":"en"},"8808":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-55","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-server#static","content":"Missing paths, non-directories, directories and InvalidPath map to 404; PermissionDenied maps to 403; other read failures map to 500. Error pages retain upstream's separate resolution path: ordinary-file exclude/containment checks and download disposition are not applied. Do not assume the ordinary-request path rules apply to error pages.","locale":"en"},"8809":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-56","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-server#static","content":"MIME selection tries content detection, filename, then octet-stream. Real fixtures cover all 171 supported MIME values from file-type 20.5.0. Detection owns an independent cancellable stream and supports small chunks and container headers beyond the first chunk. Extension mappings and picomatch exclusions are compared with upstream. Attachment filenames use UTF-8 encoding.","locale":"en"},"8810":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-57","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-server#proxy","content":"`ProxyPlugin` / `ProxyConfig` depends on `server` and `http`. Required `target` is treated as a directory. Its pathname gains a trailing slash even when the configured URL has a query or fragment. Decoded path segments are encoded again, preserving `+` and segment sub-delimiters; `?`, `#` and `%` stay path data. Normalized destinations must remain on the same origin and beneath the target directory. Escaping HTTP requests return 400; escaping WS requests are disconnected without connecting upstream or accepting downstream. Queries replace the target query using URLSearchParams encoding.","locale":"en"},"8811":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-58","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-server#proxy","content":"HTTP forwards the method and headers, with bodies only for POST/PUT/PATCH. The raw client retains 4xx/5xx and applies Fetch redirect rules to streaming bodies. Decompressed responses remove content-encoding/content-length. Upstream's returned-Response adoption behavior is retained: custom reason text is dropped and repeated Set-Cookie keeps only its last value.","locale":"en"},"8812":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-59","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-server#proxy","content":"WS connects upstream before accepting downstream, filtering Host, Connection, Upgrade, handshake key/version/extensions/accept and content-length. Text and binary messages travel in both directions. Close codes 1000 and 3000–4999 are preserved; others become 1000. Upstream transport errors close downstream with 1011. A failed upstream handshake is not accepted and is not replaced with an invented 502 response.","locale":"en"},"8813":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-60","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-server#proxy","content":"`ServerProxy` uses service key `server.proxy`. Its `public_url()` reads Server from the **providing plugin's context**, including its mount prefix; later consumer interception does not change this getter. Replacing HTTP/Server rebuilds Proxy through dependency tracking. An ordinary upstream connection failure remains operation-local, so a healthy Proxy can serve again when upstream returns.","locale":"en"},"8814":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-61","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-server#auxiliary","content":"`FsExt` adds `base_url`, `resolve`, `file` and `mount`. The configured base and every outer-to-inner intercept are directory URLs: pathname ends in `/`, query and fragment are cleared, and opaque bases such as `mem:bucket` are rejected. Thus `file:///app` → `data` → `logs?x#y` resolves beneath `file:///app/data/logs/`. On Windows, absolute drive paths become file URLs at the input boundary. Unknown configuration fields are tolerated. The longest mount prefix wins. `LocalFsPlugin` explicitly mounts file: while existing native watches remain available. This work does not port the FS write/delete/rename ecosystem.","locale":"en"},"8815":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-62","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-server#auxiliary","content":"`HttpExt::request` returns raw status, reason, headers and a streaming body. `WebSocketExt::ws` opens client connections. Configuration supports baseUrl, headers, timeout, keepAlive, proxyAgent and additional PEM rootCertificates. Interception follows upstream merging, and request headers replace configuration headers. Timeout runs until response headers arrive; the body remains owned by the caller's lifecycle. TLS uses platform verification with optional added trust roots and never disables verification.","locale":"en"},"8816":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-server-63","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-server","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-server#auxiliary","content":"Decoding supports gzip, deflate (zlib/raw), Brotli and Zstd. Raw response headers retain compression metadata so the caller can decide how to forward it. The auxiliary client does not cover the complete HTTP decoder, file/data URL or SOCKS ecosystem. Its WS client does not offer permessage-deflate; application-message semantics are compared. Production paths are Rust; Node runs only the reference tests.","locale":"en"},"8817":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-services","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-services","type":"page","content":"Services and traceability","breadcrumbs":["kardia Documentation","Cordis reference"],"tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/reference-services","locale":"en"},"8818":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-services-0","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-services","content":"How Cordis attributes effects created inside service methods to the calling fiber, using the Service base class, trackers and traceable proxies.","locale":"en"},"8819":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-services-1","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-services","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/reference-services#problem","content":"The problem it solves","locale":"en"},"8820":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-services-2","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-services","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/reference-services#service","content":"The `Service` base class","locale":"en"},"8821":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-services-3","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-services","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/reference-services#traceable-proxies","content":"Trackers and traceable proxies","locale":"en"},"8822":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-services-4","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-services","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/reference-services#associated-properties","content":"Associated properties","locale":"en"},"8823":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-services-5","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-services","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/reference-services#port","content":"Why it matters for a port","locale":"en"},"8824":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-services-6","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-services","content":"This page covers `cordis/packages/core/src/service.ts` and `utils.ts:113-238`. The paper counterparts are §5.1.4 (context access, see Implementation) and §6.3 (capabilities, see Discussion and related work). The mechanism is mostly *implicit* in the paper, but it is essential for temporal composability in practice.","locale":"en"},"8825":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-services-7","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-services#problem","content":"A service is provided by one fiber (the provider) and called by many others (consumers). When a consumer calls, say, `ctx.timer.setTimeout(cb, 100)`, the timer it creates is an effect that must be **owned by the consumer's fiber**. Otherwise unloading the consumer would leak the timer, and unloading the provider would kill timers it does not conceptually own. Likewise, `ctx.logger('x')` should be named after the calling plugin. Cordis therefore distinguishes two contexts on every service call:","locale":"en"},"8826":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-services-8","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-services#problem","content":"Site","locale":"en"},"8827":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-services-9","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-services#problem","content":"What it is","locale":"en"},"8828":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-services-10","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-services#problem","content":"Governs","locale":"en"},"8829":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-services-11","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-services#problem","content":"**use site**","locale":"en"},"8830":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-services-12","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-services#problem","content":"the context the service was accessed from (the caller)","locale":"en"},"8831":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-services-13","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-services#problem","content":"*effects*, *interception*, *isolation*","locale":"en"},"8832":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-services-14","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-services#problem","content":"**def site**","locale":"en"},"8833":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-services-15","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-services#problem","content":"the context where the accessing code was defined (the service's own context when code inside the service runs)","locale":"en"},"8834":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-services-16","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-services#problem","content":"*service resolution* (which `inject` declarations admit access)","locale":"en"},"8835":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-services-17","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-services#service","content":"`service.ts`:","locale":"en"},"8836":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-services-18","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-services#service","content":"Constructing a `Service` inside a plugin's `apply` provides it; the provision is a revertible effect of that fiber.","locale":"en"},"8837":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-services-19","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-services#service","content":"`[Service.init]()` is called after construction by the fiber runner (`fiber.ts:158-165`); its return value is treated as an effect (it may be async or a generator).","locale":"en"},"8838":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-services-20","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-services#service","content":"`[Service.check]()` is an extra availability predicate consulted by consumers' `_checkImpl`.","locale":"en"},"8839":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-services-21","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-services#service","content":"`[Service.invoke]` makes the service callable (`ctx.logger('name')`).","locale":"en"},"8840":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-services-22","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-services#service","content":"`[symbols.filter](ctx)` is the default realm-equality filter for events emitted by the service.","locale":"en"},"8841":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-services-23","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-services#service","content":"`[Service.extend](props)` creates a derived instance (a prototype child or a callable clone).","locale":"en"},"8842":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-services-24","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-services#service","content":"`[Service.mergeIntercept]` / `[Service.resolveIntercept]` handle interception metadata (see Interception).","locale":"en"},"8843":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-services-25","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-services#service","content":"`Service[Symbol.hasInstance]` walks constructors, because instances may be proxies.","locale":"en"},"8844":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-services-26","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-services#traceable-proxies","content":"Any object with a `[symbols.tracker]` descriptor `{ property?: 'ctx', associate?: string, noShadow?: boolean }` is wrapped by `getTraceable(ctx, value)` whenever it is read off a context. `createTraceable` (`utils.ts:164-220`) returns a Proxy that behaves as follows:","locale":"en"},"8845":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-services-27","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-services#traceable-proxies","content":"Access","locale":"en"},"8846":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-services-28","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-services#traceable-proxies","content":"Result","locale":"en"},"8847":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-services-29","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-services#traceable-proxies","content":"`value[tracker.property]` (i.e. `service.ctx`)","locale":"en"},"8848":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-services-30","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-services#traceable-proxies","content":"the **use-site** context","locale":"en"},"8849":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-services-31","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-services#traceable-proxies","content":"`value[symbols.caller]`","locale":"en"},"8850":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-services-32","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-services#traceable-proxies","content":"the def site (the caller's def site)","locale":"en"},"8851":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-services-33","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-services#traceable-proxies","content":"`value[symbols.original]`","locale":"en"},"8852":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-services-34","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-services#traceable-proxies","content":"the raw object","locale":"en"},"8853":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-services-35","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-services#traceable-proxies","content":"`value.foo` where `\"${associate}.foo\"` is a declared context property","locale":"en"},"8854":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-services-36","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-services#traceable-proxies","content":"the associated property (e.g. service `foo.bar` reachable as `ctx.foo.bar`; mixins into associated types such as sessions)","locale":"en"},"8855":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-services-37","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-services#traceable-proxies","content":"a getter / method","locale":"en"},"8856":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-services-38","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-services#traceable-proxies","content":"invoked with a **shadow receiver** whose `ctx` is `useSite.extend({ [symbols.shadow]: defSite })`","locale":"en"},"8857":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-services-39","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-services#traceable-proxies","content":"a nested traceable value","locale":"en"},"8858":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-services-40","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-services#traceable-proxies","content":"traced again with the use site","locale":"en"},"8859":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-services-41","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-services#traceable-proxies","content":"calling a callable service","locale":"en"},"8860":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-services-42","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-services#traceable-proxies","content":"`[symbols.invoke]` applied to the shadow receiver","locale":"en"},"8861":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-services-43","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-services#traceable-proxies","content":"The **shadow** context (`symbols.shadow`) is a pair: the object itself is the use site (its fiber receives effects), and its `shadow` property is the def site (consulted by the proxy `get` trap for `locate`). Hence, inside a service method:","locale":"en"},"8862":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-services-44","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-services#traceable-proxies","content":"`this.ctx.effect(...)` and `this.ctx.on(...)` are registered in the **caller's** fiber;","locale":"en"},"8863":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-services-45","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-services#traceable-proxies","content":"`this.ctx.otherService` is resolved against the **service's** own `inject` (the def site), even when called from root (`shadow.spec.ts: 'applies the service inject when entered from root'`);","locale":"en"},"8864":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-services-46","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-services#traceable-proxies","content":"`noShadow` services (events, logger, reflect, registry) expose the caller without preserving shadows.","locale":"en"},"8865":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-services-47","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-services#traceable-proxies","content":"`withProps` / `withProp` build lightweight proxies that override a few properties; `joinPrototype` merges a class prototype with `Function.prototype` for callable services; `createCallable` builds the function object.","locale":"en"},"8866":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-services-48","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-services#associated-properties","content":"Covered by `tests/associate.spec.ts`. A service named `foo.bar` (or a property provided as `foo.bar`) is reachable as `ctx.foo.bar`. Objects created by a service (e.g. a `Session` with tracker `associate: 'session'`) expose mixins registered under `session.*` by *other* plugins (`ctx.mixin('bar', { answer: 'session.answer' })`). Visibility follows the declaring fiber (`'associated access follows the service fiber'`).","locale":"en"},"8867":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-services-49","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-services#associated-properties","content":"This is how independent plugins extend each other's types at runtime: the dynamic counterpart of TypeScript module augmentation (paper §6.4, see Discussion and related work).","locale":"en"},"8868":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-services-50","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-services#port","content":"Concern","locale":"en"},"8869":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-services-51","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-services#port","content":"JS mechanism","locale":"en"},"8870":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-services-52","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-services#port","content":"Must be preserved as","locale":"en"},"8871":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-services-53","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-services#port","content":"Effects attributed to the caller","locale":"en"},"8872":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-services-54","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-services#port","content":"`service.ctx` returns use-site ctx via Proxy","locale":"en"},"8873":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-services-55","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-services#port","content":"service methods must receive/obtain the caller's context explicitly","locale":"en"},"8874":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-services-56","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-services#port","content":"Service resolution from def site","locale":"en"},"8875":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-services-57","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-services#port","content":"shadow context in `locate`","locale":"en"},"8876":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-services-58","page_id":"/en/docs/refere