hecked`) is its own JSON view."],[5836,"A `Service` comes back from `ctx.get` as a view whose `caller` is the accessing context (Kardia's `Svc::caller`). Views are `Proxy` objects, so a service's `#private` fields do not pass through them; use `private`."],[7336,"a `Service` under `S::NAME`"],[7143,"A `Service` value under its key"],[6601,"A `Spawner` starts the processes, so the app decides how a guest runs (a sandbox, a process group, a process manager that owns it):"],[6612,"a `tokio::sync::watch` receiver of the report (`None` while the process runs)"],[7011,"A `TreeBackend` decides where a tree's changes go. Every method has a default:"],[4792,"A binding counts as available only while the installing fiber is `ACTIVE`; a provider entering `UNLOADING` stops providing, so dependents recompute an unsatisfied target and begin their own teardown *while its bindings are still in place*."],[7530,"a binding of `key` in `realm` appears or disappears; delivered only to listeners whose context resolves `key` to that realm"],[7397,"A binding that a fiber on the way provides itself, in the same realm, is returned directly."],[4523,"A calculus of dynamic composition"],[5172,"A calculus of dynamic composition"],[4563,"A calculus with realms would relax disjointness to \"disjoint within a realm\" (§4.4, see Metatheory). The single shared realm restricts components with a non-empty provision to one fiber at a time."],[6584,"A call fails with `the guest instance is disposed` once the providing fiber is gone, with `the guest process exited` once a process guest has ended on its own, and with `invalid result of <key>.<method>: …` if the guest returns something that is not JSON. A guest calling back into an instance that is already running on the same thread (directly or through other guests) fails with `re-entrant call into a guest instance` instead of deadlocking. Like any service, a guest service is withdrawn when the guest's fiber is disposed."],[6076,"A Cargo workspace at the repository root, next to the untouched `cordis/` reference code (a local checkout of the TypeScript sources, ignored by git), `examples/`, `docs/` and the documentation site in `site/`:"],[8244,"A change from one non-⊥ target to another while `ACTIVE` goes through `UNLOADING` and then chains into `LOADING` (unload checks the epoch at its end)."],[5334,"A change to"],[6992,"a changed `name` or `inject`, or an entry that is not running: the entry is rebuilt (its fibers disposed, then started again);"],[6866,"A changed module does not load, or no longer exports a plugin"],[7095,"a char of an expression is located through YAML escapes;"],[7210,"A check or transform of the raw configuration before it is decoded"],[8239,"A child fiber is therefore exactly Definition 52: an ordinary effect of the parent whose inverse retires it. Unloading a parent cascades to its children through the parent's accumulator. Unlike the calculus, retirement and removal are fused: the inverse retires, waits for the chain to finish, and then the fiber is gone (its uid is already `null`)."],[7963,"a child plugin *inherits* the injection of an ancestor (`inject` declared on an ancestor admits access in descendants);"],[7633,"A client can close at any time, and the Rust app usually runs without any window open. So a host plugin should look a client service up when it needs it (`live_as`), rather than declare it in `inject`, which would stop that plugin whenever the window closes. The client provides its services again after every reconnect."],[7631,"A client can provide a service to the host, as an effect of the calling plugin:"],[7366,"a closure plugin"],[4658,"A coeffect at key `k` is a pair `(𝒱_k, 𝒜_k)`; each operation `a ∈ 𝒜_k` with argument `X_a` and outcome `B_a` acts **on the value alone**:"],[7164,"A compile error if `S` is not in `D`"],[7324,"A complete example"],[4884,"A component takes a configuration bound into its effect function; one component with two payloads is two components. The declarative layer may **revise** a running fiber (replace its config, reassign realms, disable or re-enable it). Disabling is O-Retire; every other revision is retire → deactivate → remove (children first) → reinsert at the same name. Re-enabling uses this composite rather than resetting `τ` to `⊥` (the monotonicity of `τ` underlies Theorem 80, and a re-enabled orphan would activate with its creator gone). In the implementation, re-enabling an *entry* instantiates a *fresh fiber*: the entry is the identity across revisions, the fiber the identity of one enablement. The shortcuts the loader takes (config handed to the component, a realm moved without reloading the provider) are justified by their *endpoint* (Theorem 80)."],[5100,"A component's loading is not one effect but a **sequence**."],[7545,"a configuration resolution hook (`ConfigHook`) for every fiber of the root: it gets the source configuration and the next stage, ultimately the plugin's own decoding. The loader uses it to evaluate expressions."],[4951,"A consumer is started only when every injected key is available; every provide/withdraw re-evaluates affected consumers."],[7962,"a consumer reads the **committed** impl, not the live store, so it keeps seeing its dependency during its own teardown (Theorem 70);"],[6684,"a crash brings down the host"],[6549,"a crashing guest fails only its own plugin"],[7100,"A custom `TreeBackend` gets the same output by implementing `locate` and `locate_expr`; `TreeHandle::locate` and `TreeHandle::locate_expr` call them for a local id."],[4573,"A deactivation cannot be one step: a consumer torn down *because its provider is leaving* runs teardown code that may need that very provider (for example, returning connections to a pool). So the **decision** is separated from the **act**, and the act is guarded."],[5956,"A declarative **loader** (entries, groups, realms) and file-backed **include** with two-way sync."],[7952,"a declared **accessor** → its getter;"],[7248,"A decoding error names the failing path, such as `invalid config: … (at items.1)`."],[4995,"A dependency cycle leaves the involved components **permanently inactive**. Unlike a concurrency deadlock it is *predictable from the declarations*, so a runtime can report it at load time. Most cycles decompose: for example, server ↔ access-controller becomes `server-core`, `access-control-core`, `request-mediation` (depending on both cores) and `policy-management` (depending on both). The cost is a potentially quadratic number of integration components, mitigated by package bundling, convention-based wiring and scaffolding."],[6998,"a dependency loss unloads an entry's outer fiber without disposing it; the entry then reads as disabled until its outer fiber is reactivated, and nothing is written."],[6654,"a dependency: a module path relative to this module"],[4820,"A dependent gains or loses the binding exactly when the move separates it from the provider."],[5336,"a docs page (`site/`)"],[7630,"A failed call rejects with a `BridgeError` whose `type` is `unavailable`, `timeout`, `invalid` or `failed`."],[8268,"A failed fiber **does not re-enter** on a dependency refresh (`_setEpoch` returns early). This matches §4.4: \"not retried against an unchanged environment\"."],[5465,"A failing effect body disposes what it collected; synchronous errors propagate to the caller, async ones are logged and reported via `ready()`."],[5486,"A faithful port of `_checkImpl` / `_refresh` / `_setEpoch` / `_reload` / `_unload` / `_updateState`, split into pure critical sections returning actions:"],[4865,"A fiber begins a transition only where its dependencies are provided (`L-Begin(m) ⇒ γ ⊧ d_m`)."],[7527,"a fiber changes state"],[4544,"A fiber instantiating `(d, p, e)` is `⟨d, p, e, π, σ, τ, θ⟩`:"],[7521,"a fiber is created (`internal/plugin` on insertion)"],[7536,"a fiber is moved to another plugin implementation by `registry.replace`"],[7524,"a fiber is retired (`internal/plugin` on removal)"],[5852,