,"Dispatching"],[7623,"Disposal cancels future triggers, then waits for callbacks already in flight. A timeout's effect remains until its callback finishes. Managed callbacks therefore finish before the old instance's cleanup completes. Callbacks must return the Promise for their asynchronous work; detached tasks and native timers are outside this mechanism. Do not await disposal of the callback's own effect or owning fiber from inside that callback: it would wait for itself. Report an instance failure through the non-waiting `fail()` operation instead."],[5620,"Disposal order"],[6359,"Disposal order and errors"],[7301,"Dispose a returned `Route` / `WsRoute` to unregister it individually; plugin disposal also releases them. Pending HTTP callbacks and response sources are cancelled and awaited, and a WS route closes its connections. User callbacks and asynchronous cleanup run outside runtime locks."],[1164,"dispose 返回前 pending 回调、body 源、中间件、route hook、upgrade hook 均销毁"],[8029,"Disposers are **initiated in reverse order but run concurrently**; each starts after a microtask. Inside a single effect they are sequential LIFO. The paper acknowledges this (\"`fiber.dispose` initiates a fiber's effects concurrently\", §5.1.3), and it is sound under the independence results (Theorem 43: inverses of independent effects can run in any order; see Metatheory)."],[4887,"Disposers must revert exactly their effect (author obligation, unchecked)"],[5118,"Disposers of a composite effect run in reverse order of their creation."],[6435,"disposes them all **concurrently**, logging every failure and panic;"],[7134,"Disposing `db` withdraws `database`: `greeter` stops, and its interval and the `closing` log line are reverted and run as part of the teardown."],[8270,"Disposing a `PENDING` or `FAILED` fiber settles it to `DISPOSED`."],[5124,"Disposing twice is forbidden: an inverse applied at a state no application produced reverts nothing meaningful (the implementation enforces \"at most on