ows Theorem 73 (Metatheory): the provider graph is traversed on demand."],[5694,"Test category"],[5856,"Testing strategy"],[7733,"Testing strategy"],[7983,"Tests documenting the semantics"],[5689,"Tests inject errors, panics or Fiber failure into the actual implementation and observe real sockets and cleanup counters. Automatic recovery does not use test-driven updates. Manual mode is observed Failed before explicit handling. Host mode follows callback decisions. Only the failed instance and consumers of unavailable services may be interrupted."],[5253,"Tests run on a `current_thread` runtime with `tokio::time::pause()` for deterministic interleavings (the analogue of vitest fake timers); production may use the multi-threaded runtime. Testing describes which runtime each test suite uses today."],[5871,"Tests that depend on timers: the timer suite, and the core and loader tests whose effects wait on timers."],[4659,"That is, an effect function on the value plus an outcome. Its lift to `Σ` (eq. 31) reads and writes only the binding at `k`: typing confines the operation to its key. An operation that depends on another key must read that key's value into its argument. Operations induce an equivalence `≃_k` (§3.3.2, below)."],[7437,"that metadata merged with `S::merge_intercept`"],[7569,"The @kardiajs npm packages: the same plugin model in TypeScript, the bridge that connects a TypeScript runtime, such as a Tauri webview, to a Rust kardia app, and extension points for Vue interfaces."],[4704,"The **commuting part** of a computation is carried by effects: performed in any order, reverted in any order (Theorem 43). The **order-sensitive part** is carried by coeffects: within one component the accumulator imposes LIFO; across components a declared coeffect imposes \"provider before consumer\". Composability is therefore obtained at the grain of components, not of single effects."],[4724,"the **component loader**: configuration reconciliation and HMR (§5.2);"],[4723,"the **core library**: effect and coeffect systems (§5.1);"],[6896,"The **loader** (`kardia-loader`) keeps a tree of **entries** (a plugin name plus its configuration) in step with live fibers, in both directions: changes to the entries are reconciled into the running fibers, and a plugin that updates or disposes itself is written back to its entry. The **include** plugin (`kardia-include`) grafts the entries of a YAML or JSON file into that tree and keeps the file in sync both ways."],[8505,"The **outer fiber** carries the entry-level dependencies; the **inner fiber** is the actual plugin. If a dependency disappears during the import, the async-iterator guard stops at the `yield` and the plugin never starts (an L-Divert at an iteration boundary). Losing a dependency later unloads the outer fiber, which disposes the inner one as a child effect; when the dependency returns, the outer fiber reloads and re-imports."],[7929,"the **provider-side guard**: `await` until `impl.dependents` is empty (`impl.release` is resolved by the last dependent's unload). It always yields at least once, so child cleanup can still access the inherited service;"],[5941,"the **reference implementation**: the Cordis reference section (TypeScript, Cordis v4),"],[8861,"The **shadow** context (`symbols.shadow`) is a pair: the object itself is the use site (its fiber receives effects), and its `shadow` property is the def site (consulted by the proxy `get` trap for `locate`). Hence, inside a service method:"],[5940,"the **theory**: the Concepts section (the paper's definitions, rules and theorems), and"],[8848,"the **use-site** context"],[8154,"The *return value* of `apply` is treated as an effect (see Effects): a function, a promise, or a (async) generator of disposers."],[5360,"The `@kardiajs/*` packages share one version, apart from the crates' (they start at 0.1.0). The bridge's protocol has a version of its own (`PROTOCOL` on both sides): a client opening a session with another version is refused with an `invalid` error, so a host and a client from different releases fail clearly instead of misbehaving."],[6116,"the `#[derive(Service)]` / `#[plugin]` proc macros"],[7429,"the `Arc<T>`"],[8546,"The `disabled` verdict is cached; it is re-evaluated on start, on an explicit update or on outer reactivation."],[5550,"The `DisposableList` design (insert returns a fresh id; remove by id; `drain_rev()` returns LIFO order) is exported publicly as `kardia_core::IdTable<T>`. It is what paper §3.4.2 calls a commutative key: service authors should build their registries (routes, listeners, commands) on it."],[6936,"the `EntryHandle` of a (composite) id"],[8137,"The `Fiber` data structure"],[6362,"The `Fiber` handle"],[7122,"The `Fiber` handle"],[6524,"The `Guest` trait"],[6610,"the `GuestExit` report, once the process has ended"],[6583,"the `GuestHandle` of a process guest"],[6525,"The `Host`"],[6893,"The `Include` handle"],[7038,"The `include` plugin (`kardia_include::IncludePlugin`) loads the entries of a YAML or JSON file as a subtree and keeps the file in sync both ways. It declares the `loader` service; with `watch` it also needs `fs`."],[8120,"the `internal/config` validator."],[8118,"the `internal/listener` handler that turns `ctx.on('internal/update', …)` into a per-fiber hook list (`fiber._hooks['internal/update']`, itself a tracked effect);"],[7761,"The `kardia` binary loads an app from a config file:"],[7804,"The `kardia` binary loads an app from a YAML (or JSON) config file and keeps it in step with the file: editing the file reconfigures the running app, and changes the app makes to its own entries are written back."],[6569,"The `kardia` binary picks a backend by file extension: `.wasm` and `.wat` go to `WasmSource`, anything else to `DylibSource`. An app built on the library chooses its own:"],[6819,"The `kardia` binary registers `hmr` (also as `@cordisjs/plugin-hmr`) and resolves any entry name that is not a built-in plugin as a guest module relative to the config file, so `examples/kardia.yml` hot-reloads `plugins/hello.wat`:"],[5827,"The `kardia` binary registers `hmr` and resolves any entry name that is not a built-in plugin as a guest module relative to the config file: `examples/kardia.yml` hot-reloads `plugins/hello.wat`."],[6146,"The `kardia` binary runs the app described by a YAML or JSON config file (`kardia.yml` by default). The file is a list of **entries**. Each entry runs one plugin:"],[8725,"The `loader { await: true }` barrier."],[8588,"The `loader/patch-context` listener implements Algorithm 7:"],[5812,"The `loader/patch-context` waterfall is `Loader::on_patch_context(ctx, hook)`: a hook gets the entry and the rest of the chain as a future, which builds the rest only when awaited; hooks run in registration order inside the loader's own isolation step and are removed with `ctx`'s fiber. Unlike TS, the new isolation table is installed together with the re-parenting at the end of the chain, not before the hooks run."],[5105,"The `Maybe(ℑ)` continuation makes a **boundary** available between any two iterations: at a boundary the accumulator recovers exactly the iterations so far. This is the hook used later for *interruption* (L-Divert, see the calculus) and *cancellation*."],[6895,"The `on_patch_context` hook"],[8820,"The `Service` base class"],[5409,"The `Service` trait"],[7958,"The `set` trap: an undeclared property on a non-root fiber → the error `cannot set property without provide`; an accessor → its setter; a service → `waterfall('internal/set')` → `reflect.set`."],[5668,"The `Svc` caller owns route, mount, connection and stream effects; services resolve dependencies at their definition site. In-flight HTTP handlers, middleware, route/upgrade hooks and response sources belong to cancellable task collections. Disposal unregisters, stops and awaits destruction before recovery creates another instance. Streams use bounded channels; reader/poll panics become operation errors."],[5535,"The `type_id` check refers to paper §6.6."],[7854,"The `Watcher` is an async effect wrapping chokidar (`ignoreInitial`), registered after the initial scan. Only `change` events are forwarded; `ready` replays asynchronously to late listeners; dispatch uses a snapshot; listener errors go to `error` listeners (whose own errors are only logged)."],[5290,"The `xtask` crate (`xtask/`, a workspace member that is never published) holds the repository's own tasks; `.cargo/config.toml` makes them `cargo xtask <command>`."],[8985,"The `yakumo` build orchestrator (`yakumo.yml`: `yakumo`, `yakumo-tsc`, `yakumo-esbuild`, `yakumo-vitest`): `build` = esbuild bundles + `tsc` declarations; `test` = vitest; coverage variants."],[7077,"the absolute path of the file"],[7423,"the accessing `Context`"],[5103,"The accumulator becomes `φ ∘ g₁ ∘ … ∘ g_k`, which reverts in LIFO order (Theorem 16)."],[6070,"The accumulator is `FiberData::disposables: IdTable<Arc<EffectCell>>`; there is no `EffectId` type (`IdTable` hands out `u64` ids)."],[8897,"The analysed code is the TypeScript checkout at `cordis/` (a separate git repository, ignored by the outer repository's `.gitignore`), at HEAD `6663348 fix(fs-node): reject drive-relative paths on Windows (#285)`. The `cordis` core package is version `4.0.0-rc.12`, the paper's \"Cordis v4\". File:line references (`reflect.ts:182-185`) are against that revision."],[7625,"The app creates a `Bridge`, mounts its plugin, and routes client requests into `Bridge::handle`. Plugins inject `bridge` and expose services; an exposure is an effect of the exposing plugin, so it is withdrawn when that plugin stops:"],[8854,"the associated property (e.g. service `foo.bar` reachable as `ctx.foo.bar`; mixins into associated types such as sessions)"],[6623,"The backends implement two public traits, which a host can implement for a transport of its own:"],[5405,"The base path is only for GitHub Pages, which serves the site under `/kardia/`; without `VITE_BASE_PATH`, the build serves from `/`."],[6279,"The binary starts `fs` and `fs:local` itself and includes the config file with `watch: true`, so the file is an `include` whose entries form the app."],[7151,"The binding is withdrawn when the plugin stops. Its consumers stop first."],[5472,"The boundaries are synchronous in the code (`scope.yield_(..)?`, `ctx.checkpoint()?`). `ctx.checkpoint()` fails once the load has been superseded (the fiber's target no longer equals the target of its episode) or the fiber was retired; `scope.checkpoint()` fails once the effect has been disposed. Async effect bodies are spawned and never dropped half-way; disposal waits for the body to reach its end or a boundary."],[4971,"The boundary divides the environment into locations **inside** (the system can modify them exclusively *and* restore them, so they are tracked and revertible) and **outside** (either ability fails, so the operation acts as `id_Γ` and is untracked)."],[7574,"The bridge"],[6622,"The bridge emits two events on the guest's fiber: `GuestStarted { fiber, handle }` once the process has started, before `apply`, and `GuestExited { fiber, exit }` when it ends before the fiber disposes it. The fiber then fails with the report as its error (`Fiber::fail`), which withdraws its services, so their consumers wait for them again. As for any failed fiber, `restart` keeps it failed; `fiber.update(config)` starts a new process."],[5855,"The bridge's calls are asynchronous, unlike the guest ABI's (Dynamic modules): it is the asynchronous contract of paper §6.2. Its end-to-end tests drive the real Rust host from the TypeScript client."],[7591,"The bridge's transport over one Tauri command and a `Channel`"],[4978,"The broker enables **load balancing** (policy-driven dispatch; scale by loading and unloading providers), **rolling updates** (load the new provider, shift traffic, unload the old one once drained: quiescence/tranquility), and **cross-process invocation** (each process hosts its own context; RPC preserves the interface; it must be designed against an *asynchronous* contract)."],[7849,"The browser build delegates to `console.*`."],[5288,"The build copies the repository's `LICENSE` and `js/NOTICE` (the MIT notice of Cordis) into each package, since npm only ships files inside a package directory; the copies are gitignored."],[7712,"The calculus: components, fibers, the nine rules (§4.1–4.2)"],[6579,"the call off the async workers, with an optional timeout; dropping the future cancels the call (a `kardia-guest/2` guest is told, and its result is dropped)"],[6778,"the caller no longer wants export `id`: the guest may stop early; the host drops its result and answers its later host calls with an error"],[7298,"The catalog also accepts the actual upstream names: `@cordisjs/server`, `@cordisjs/server-acl`, `@cordisjs/server-echo`, `@cordisjs/server-static`, `@cordisjs/server-proxy`, `@cordisjs/http`, `@cordisjs/fs` and `@cordisjs/fs-node`."],[7174,"The checked accesses read the plugin's **committed view**: the bindings it resolved when it started. Access walks from the plugin's own provisions up through its ancestors, stops at an isolation boundary, and returns the first binding the plugin committed to. Because consumers read their committed view rather than the live store, a dependency stays readable while the consumer itself is being torn down (Theorem 70 in Metatheory). The root context has unchecked access to the live store."],[7629,"The client connects in the background and reconnects when the host's bridge restarts. Its plugin is active while disconnected; host services are simply absent. `bridge.status` and the `bridge/status` event report `connecting`, `connected`, `disconnected` or `replaced` (another webview with the same label took over; it does not reconnect)."],[7589,"The client side of the bridge"],[5047,"The closest precedent, but with hand-written, synchronous deactivation callbacks. Cordis reverts automatically and runs asynchronous teardown to completion (inertial